73
00:02:47,360 --> 00:02:51,120
The story of X-ray discovery is actually

74
00:02:49,120 --> 00:02:54,160
a wonderful example of serendipity in

75
00:02:51,120 --> 00:02:56,480
science. In 1,895,

76
00:02:54,160 --> 00:02:59,040
Wilhelm Conrad Ronkin was experimenting

77
00:02:56,480 --> 00:03:01,040
with a cathode ray tube, essentially an

78
00:02:59,040 --> 00:03:03,360
early electron gun, when he noticed

79
00:03:01,040 --> 00:03:05,599
something unexpected.

80
00:03:03,360 --> 00:03:07,680
A nearby fluorescent screen began to

81
00:03:05,599 --> 00:03:10,400
glow, even though the tube was enclosed

82
00:03:07,680 --> 00:03:12,560
in a box. By accident, he had discovered

83
00:03:10,400 --> 00:03:14,720
a new kind of invisible ray that could

84
00:03:12,560 --> 00:03:17,360
pass through many opaque materials. He

85
00:03:14,720 --> 00:03:20,239
called it the X-ray, where the letter X

86
00:03:17,360 --> 00:03:22,159
simply meant unknown. One of the first

87
00:03:20,239 --> 00:03:24,400
images he captured was of his wife's

88
00:03:22,159 --> 00:03:26,319
hand showing her bones and even her

89
00:03:24,400 --> 00:03:28,000
wedding ring. That single picture

90
00:03:26,319 --> 00:03:29,440
immediately revealed the enormous

91
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:30,959
potential of this discovery for

92
00:03:29,440 --> 00:03:33,360
medicine.

93
00:03:30,959 --> 00:03:35,360
Ronin's work was recognized worldwide

94
00:03:33,360 --> 00:03:37,280
and in 1,91

95
00:03:35,360 --> 00:03:39,680
he was awarded the very first Nobel

96
00:03:37,280 --> 00:03:41,440
Prize in physics.

97
00:03:39,680 --> 00:03:42,879
So from a chance observation in the

98
00:03:41,440 --> 00:03:44,959
laboratory came one of the most

99
00:03:42,879 --> 00:03:48,200
important tools in medical diagnosis,

100
00:03:44,959 --> 00:03:48,200
the X-ray.

101
00:03:49,519 --> 00:03:54,000
Ronin made his discovery while

102
00:03:51,200 --> 00:03:55,599
experimenting with a cathode tube. At

103
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:57,760
that time he didn't know what was

104
00:03:55,599 --> 00:04:00,560
happening. Nobody did. He placed the

105
00:03:57,760 --> 00:04:02,959
tube inside a box and unexpectedly a

106
00:04:00,560 --> 00:04:05,040
nearby fluorescent screen lit up. When

107
00:04:02,959 --> 00:04:06,959
he looked closer he noticed that objects

108
00:04:05,040 --> 00:04:08,720
could block or shape this invisible

109
00:04:06,959 --> 00:04:11,040
radiation.

110
00:04:08,720 --> 00:04:13,120
For example, a metallic cross placed in

111
00:04:11,040 --> 00:04:14,560
front of the tube cast a clear shadow on

112
00:04:13,120 --> 00:04:16,880
the screen. This was something

113
00:04:14,560 --> 00:04:18,160
completely new. It couldn't be explained

114
00:04:16,880 --> 00:04:19,919
without assuming that some kind of

115
00:04:18,160 --> 00:04:21,440
invisible physical rays were being

116
00:04:19,919 --> 00:04:22,960
produced.

117
00:04:21,440 --> 00:04:25,520
Because the nature of these rays was

118
00:04:22,960 --> 00:04:27,919
unknown, he called them X-rays. Later,

119
00:04:25,520 --> 00:04:30,320
they became known as Ronin rays in honor

120
00:04:27,919 --> 00:04:32,240
of his discovery.

121
00:04:30,320 --> 00:04:34,720
This breakthrough was so important that

122
00:04:32,240 --> 00:04:36,880
Ronkin was awarded the very first Nobel

123
00:04:34,720 --> 00:04:38,479
Prize in physics. Today, of course, we

124
00:04:36,880 --> 00:04:40,320
understand X-rays in much greater

125
00:04:38,479 --> 00:04:44,520
detail, but the original observation was

126
00:04:40,320 --> 00:04:44,520
truly remarkable for its time.

127
00:04:45,600 --> 00:04:50,080
X-rays are a special type of

128
00:04:47,360 --> 00:04:52,160
electromagnetic radiation. They occupy a

129
00:04:50,080 --> 00:04:53,919
specific portion of the electromagnetic

130
00:04:52,160 --> 00:04:55,520
spectrum.

131
00:04:53,919 --> 00:04:57,840
The part of the spectrum we are most

132
00:04:55,520 --> 00:04:59,919
familiar with is visible light because

133
00:04:57,840 --> 00:05:01,919
we can see it with our eyes. But just

134
00:04:59,919 --> 00:05:04,080
because we cannot see other forms of

135
00:05:01,919 --> 00:05:06,240
electromagnetic radiation does not mean

136
00:05:04,080 --> 00:05:08,320
they don't exist. Think about your

137
00:05:06,240 --> 00:05:10,080
smartphone. You don't see the

138
00:05:08,320 --> 00:05:12,400
electromagnetic waves, but you

139
00:05:10,080 --> 00:05:14,639
constantly receive emails, messages, and

140
00:05:12,400 --> 00:05:16,479
photos from anywhere in the world. These

141
00:05:14,639 --> 00:05:18,240
are all carried by electromagnetic

142
00:05:16,479 --> 00:05:19,919
radiation.

143
00:05:18,240 --> 00:05:22,639
Electromagnetic waves are beautifully

144
00:05:19,919 --> 00:05:24,960
described by Maxwell's equations. They

145
00:05:22,639 --> 00:05:27,120
are sinosoidal waves traveling through

146
00:05:24,960 --> 00:05:28,800
space with an electric field and a

147
00:05:27,120 --> 00:05:30,960
magnetic field that are perpendicular to

148
00:05:28,800 --> 00:05:32,720
each other. Together they form the

149
00:05:30,960 --> 00:05:36,960
foundation for how light and all

150
00:05:32,720 --> 00:05:39,199
electromagnetic radiation behave.

151
00:05:36,960 --> 00:05:40,639
Now across this wide spectrum different

152
00:05:39,199 --> 00:05:42,240
portions can be used for different

153
00:05:40,639 --> 00:05:44,000
purposes.

154
00:05:42,240 --> 00:05:46,800
For example, X-rays are used for

155
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:48,800
radiography and CT imaging. Gamma rays

156
00:05:46,800 --> 00:05:51,280
are used in nuclear imaging like PET

157
00:05:48,800 --> 00:05:53,600
inspect. Infrared and visible light are

158
00:05:51,280 --> 00:05:55,759
used for optical imaging and pathology.

159
00:05:53,600 --> 00:05:58,479
Radio waves and microwaves are used in

160
00:05:55,759 --> 00:06:00,479
magnetic resonance imaging. So depending

161
00:05:58,479 --> 00:06:02,000
on the wavelength and frequency each

162
00:06:00,479 --> 00:06:04,400
portion of the spectrum serves a

163
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:05,840
different role in medicine.

164
00:06:04,400 --> 00:06:08,400
To describe the energy of

165
00:06:05,840 --> 00:06:12,080
electromagnetic radiation we use a

166
00:06:08,400 --> 00:06:15,440
fundamental relationship. E= H * F which

167
00:06:12,080 --> 00:06:18,240
is also equal to HC over lambda.

168
00:06:15,440 --> 00:06:20,720
Here h is plank's constant. F is

169
00:06:18,240 --> 00:06:22,960
frequency. C is the speed of light and

170
00:06:20,720 --> 00:06:24,960
lambda is the wavelength. From this

171
00:06:22,960 --> 00:06:27,199
equation, you can see that energy is

172
00:06:24,960 --> 00:06:30,240
directly proportional to frequency and

173
00:06:27,199 --> 00:06:32,160
inversely proportional to wavelength.

174
00:06:30,240 --> 00:06:33,840
In medical imaging, we work with very

175
00:06:32,160 --> 00:06:36,240
small wavelengths, usually measured in

176
00:06:33,840 --> 00:06:39,680
nanometers and angstroms. One nanometer

177
00:06:36,240 --> 00:06:41,280
is 10 the^ of - 9 m. One angstrom

178
00:06:39,680 --> 00:06:43,759
written as a small circle above the

179
00:06:41,280 --> 00:06:47,199
letter A is even smaller, 10 the power

180
00:06:43,759 --> 00:06:49,280
of - 10 m.

181
00:06:47,199 --> 00:06:50,960
So this slide shows you clearly where

182
00:06:49,280 --> 00:06:52,720
X-rays fit in the electromagnetic

183
00:06:50,960 --> 00:06:57,160
spectrum and why their energy levels

184
00:06:52,720 --> 00:06:57,160
make them so useful for medical imaging.

185
00:06:58,479 --> 00:07:02,000
If we narrow down within the

186
00:06:59,919 --> 00:07:03,919
electromagnetic spectrum, we can clearly

187
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:05,840
see where X-rays sit. Their wavelengths

188
00:07:03,919 --> 00:07:08,800
are extremely small, ranging roughly

189
00:07:05,840 --> 00:07:11,120
from 1/10enth of a nimeter up to 10 nm.

190
00:07:08,800 --> 00:07:13,680
Within this narrow window, we classify

191
00:07:11,120 --> 00:07:15,599
radiation as X-rays.

192
00:07:13,680 --> 00:07:17,759
But not all X-rays are the same. When

193
00:07:15,599 --> 00:07:20,400
the frequency is higher and remember

194
00:07:17,759 --> 00:07:22,720
higher frequency means higher energy we

195
00:07:20,400 --> 00:07:24,560
call them hard X-rays. When the

196
00:07:22,720 --> 00:07:27,039
frequency is lower corresponding to

197
00:07:24,560 --> 00:07:30,160
longer wavelengths the energy is weaker

198
00:07:27,039 --> 00:07:32,080
and we call them soft X-rays.

199
00:07:30,160 --> 00:07:34,080
Different kinds of X-rays are useful for

200
00:07:32,080 --> 00:07:36,000
different applications.

201
00:07:34,080 --> 00:07:38,000
For example, in mimography we use

202
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:40,240
relatively soft X-rays because we want

203
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:41,919
sensitivity to soft tissue structures.

204
00:07:40,240 --> 00:07:44,240
But if we want to penetrate thicker

205
00:07:41,919 --> 00:07:47,120
parts of the body or denser tissues like

206
00:07:44,240 --> 00:07:49,440
bone, we need hard X-rays with energies

207
00:07:47,120 --> 00:07:50,960
that can go above 100 kilo electron

208
00:07:49,440 --> 00:07:52,960
volts.

209
00:07:50,960 --> 00:07:55,680
In non-medical settings, even higher

210
00:07:52,960 --> 00:07:57,599
energy X-rays are used.

211
00:07:55,680 --> 00:08:00,000
For example, in airport security

212
00:07:57,599 --> 00:08:02,960
screening, luggage may be very thick or

213
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:05,520
contain dense objects. In those cases,

214
00:08:02,960 --> 00:08:07,680
stronger X-rays are required. Otherwise,

215
00:08:05,520 --> 00:08:10,720
all the radiation would be absorbed and

216
00:08:07,680 --> 00:08:12,560
no useful information would come out.

217
00:08:10,720 --> 00:08:14,800
So depending on the wavelength and

218
00:08:12,560 --> 00:08:16,879
energy, X-rays can be tuned for very

219
00:08:14,800 --> 00:08:18,960
different purposes from delicate imaging

220
00:08:16,879 --> 00:08:21,960
in medicine to powerful scanning and

221
00:08:18,960 --> 00:08:21,960
security.

222
00:08:23,360 --> 00:08:26,960
We saw this briefly back in the first

223
00:08:25,039 --> 00:08:29,199
lecture, but let's look again. This is a

224
00:08:26,960 --> 00:08:31,120
standard X-ray radiograph, in this case,

225
00:08:29,199 --> 00:08:33,039
a chest X-ray. It's a single

226
00:08:31,120 --> 00:08:36,159
two-dimensional projection of the body

227
00:08:33,039 --> 00:08:37,919
captured on film or in digital form.

228
00:08:36,159 --> 00:08:40,880
What you see here is everything

229
00:08:37,919 --> 00:08:43,039
superimposed. the heart, the lungs, the

230
00:08:40,880 --> 00:08:45,040
ribs, all layered on top of each other

231
00:08:43,039 --> 00:08:47,519
in the same image. Because of that

232
00:08:45,040 --> 00:08:49,760
overlap, radiography is not always the

233
00:08:47,519 --> 00:08:53,760
clearest view. Still, it provides

234
00:08:49,760 --> 00:08:55,600
valuable insight into the human body.

235
00:08:53,760 --> 00:08:57,279
For example, if a patient has a broken

236
00:08:55,600 --> 00:08:59,360
bone or if there is a foreign object

237
00:08:57,279 --> 00:09:01,120
like a bullet, it shows up immediately.

238
00:08:59,360 --> 00:09:03,839
That's why radiography is so important

239
00:09:01,120 --> 00:09:06,000
in emergency rooms. It's fast, widely

240
00:09:03,839 --> 00:09:07,839
available, and gives doctors a quick

241
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:09,839
look inside.

242
00:09:07,839 --> 00:09:11,920
But keep in mind this is only a

243
00:09:09,839 --> 00:09:14,000
projected view. Moving from projection

244
00:09:11,920 --> 00:09:16,640
imaging like radiography to

245
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:18,240
cross-sectional imaging like CT was a

246
00:09:16,640 --> 00:09:21,640
huge leap forward in the field of

247
00:09:18,240 --> 00:09:21,640
medical imaging.

248
00:09:23,360 --> 00:09:29,839
Here we have X-ray computed tomography

249
00:09:25,920 --> 00:09:31,920
or CT. Unlike a simple radioraph, CT

250
00:09:29,839 --> 00:09:34,000
allows us to see a clear cross-sectional

251
00:09:31,920 --> 00:09:36,320
view of the body. With CT, the

252
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:38,800
resolution can be extremely fine down to

253
00:09:36,320 --> 00:09:40,720
about 300 microns, which is 3/10en of a

254
00:09:38,800 --> 00:09:42,800
millimeter. That means we can visualize

255
00:09:40,720 --> 00:09:45,440
very small structures and details inside

256
00:09:42,800 --> 00:09:47,839
the body. How do we actually make a

257
00:09:45,440 --> 00:09:50,080
cross-sectional image from X-rays? That

258
00:09:47,839 --> 00:09:52,000
is the central question of CT and it

259
00:09:50,080 --> 00:09:53,600
will be the focus of our next lecture.

260
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:56,880
For now, I just want to give you a sense

261
00:09:53,600 --> 00:09:58,480
of how powerful this technology is. When

262
00:09:56,880 --> 00:10:00,480
you see images like this, you realize

263
00:09:58,480 --> 00:10:02,000
how useful X-ray imaging can be.

264
00:10:00,480 --> 00:10:03,760
Hopefully, this motivates you to dig

265
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:05,440
deeper to understand how the X-ray

266
00:10:03,760 --> 00:10:07,519
source, the detectors, and the

267
00:10:05,440 --> 00:10:09,279
reconstruction process all work together

268
00:10:07,519 --> 00:10:13,320
to extract as much information as

269
00:10:09,279 --> 00:10:13,320
possible from the human body.

270
00:10:14,560 --> 00:10:18,880
Looking into the future, X-ray imaging

271
00:10:16,959 --> 00:10:21,360
may become far more portable and

272
00:10:18,880 --> 00:10:24,160
accessible. Imagine carrying a powerful

273
00:10:21,360 --> 00:10:26,320
but compact X-ray device, perhaps even

274
00:10:24,160 --> 00:10:28,640
something integrated into a smartphone.

275
00:10:26,320 --> 00:10:30,240
It may sound futuristic, but nothing is

276
00:10:28,640 --> 00:10:31,839
stopping us from thinking outside the

277
00:10:30,240 --> 00:10:33,920
