458
00:17:19,280 --> 00:17:23,199
At one end, we have a filament which is

459
00:17:21,439 --> 00:17:25,039
usually made of tungsten wire. The

460
00:17:23,199 --> 00:17:27,280
filament is coiled so that it has more

461
00:17:25,039 --> 00:17:28,880
surface area and when we pass a strong

462
00:17:27,280 --> 00:17:31,360
current through it, the wire becomes

463
00:17:28,880 --> 00:17:33,600
extremely hot. This heating process

464
00:17:31,360 --> 00:17:37,280
frees up electrons. They become boiled

465
00:17:33,600 --> 00:17:39,200
off from the surface of the filament.

466
00:17:37,280 --> 00:17:41,039
Next, we apply a very high voltage

467
00:17:39,200 --> 00:17:43,360
potential between the filament, which

468
00:17:41,039 --> 00:17:46,160
serves as the cathode and the target,

469
00:17:43,360 --> 00:17:48,640
which is called the anode.

470
00:17:46,160 --> 00:17:50,480
The anode is also made of tungsten. The

471
00:17:48,640 --> 00:17:52,799
electric field between the cathode and

472
00:17:50,480 --> 00:17:54,559
the anode accelerates the electrons at

473
00:17:52,799 --> 00:17:57,679
very high speeds toward the tungsten

474
00:17:54,559 --> 00:17:59,600
target. When these high energy electrons

475
00:17:57,679 --> 00:18:02,240
strike the anode, their interactions

476
00:17:59,600 --> 00:18:04,640
with the tungsten atoms produce X-rays.

477
00:18:02,240 --> 00:18:07,919
This process also generates an enormous

478
00:18:04,640 --> 00:18:10,320
amount of heat. In fact, more than 99%

479
00:18:07,919 --> 00:18:14,480
of the energy is converted into heat and

480
00:18:10,320 --> 00:18:16,160
less than 1% actually becomes X-rays.

481
00:18:14,480 --> 00:18:18,240
Managing this heat is a major

482
00:18:16,160 --> 00:18:20,080
engineering challenge.

483
00:18:18,240 --> 00:18:22,080
The inside of the tube is kept under a

484
00:18:20,080 --> 00:18:23,679
high vacuum so that electrons can move

485
00:18:22,080 --> 00:18:26,559
freely without colliding with air

486
00:18:23,679 --> 00:18:28,559
molecules. But even in a vacuum, the

487
00:18:26,559 --> 00:18:31,360
tungsten target would quickly overheat

488
00:18:28,559 --> 00:18:34,000
if it were not carefully designed.

489
00:18:31,360 --> 00:18:36,160
The solution is to use a rotating anode.

490
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:38,160
By spinning the target rapidly, the

491
00:18:36,160 --> 00:18:40,400
electron beam does not strike the same

492
00:18:38,160 --> 00:18:42,400
spot continuously. Instead, the heat is

493
00:18:40,400 --> 00:18:45,520
spread over a larger area, preventing

494
00:18:42,400 --> 00:18:47,679
the tungsten from melting. Modern X-ray

495
00:18:45,520 --> 00:18:49,760
tubes are sealed inside durable glass or

496
00:18:47,679 --> 00:18:52,480
ceramic housings and are manufactured

497
00:18:49,760 --> 00:18:54,400
with extremely high precision.

498
00:18:52,480 --> 00:18:56,400
My colleagues at J Global Research, for

499
00:18:54,400 --> 00:18:58,960
example, have worked on these tubes for

500
00:18:56,400 --> 00:19:00,559
many years, refining the design step by

501
00:18:58,960 --> 00:19:02,240
step.

502
00:19:00,559 --> 00:19:04,000
While the basic principle has remained

503
00:19:02,240 --> 00:19:05,360
the same since Ronchin's time,

504
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:07,520
continuous improvements have made the

505
00:19:05,360 --> 00:19:09,039
tubes more efficient, more powerful, and

506
00:19:07,520 --> 00:19:10,720
capable of delivering the high flux of

507
00:19:09,039 --> 00:19:12,240
X-rays needed in today's imaging

508
00:19:10,720 --> 00:19:14,000
systems.

509
00:19:12,240 --> 00:19:15,760
So, in summary, heat the filament,

510
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:17,760
release electrons, accelerate them with

511
00:19:15,760 --> 00:19:19,679
high voltage toward a tungsten anode,

512
00:19:17,760 --> 00:19:22,679
and X-rays are generated at the point of

513
00:19:19,679 --> 00:19:22,679
impact.

514
00:19:24,160 --> 00:19:29,120
Now let's ask the question why is it

515
00:19:26,640 --> 00:19:30,640
that an X-ray tube can actually generate

516
00:19:29,120 --> 00:19:32,880
X-rays?

517
00:19:30,640 --> 00:19:35,200
The answer lies in two main physical

518
00:19:32,880 --> 00:19:38,880
mechanisms. This is what I call our

519
00:19:35,200 --> 00:19:40,240
first red diamond or key concept.

520
00:19:38,880 --> 00:19:42,720
The first mechanism is called

521
00:19:40,240 --> 00:19:45,360
brestralung radiation. A German word

522
00:19:42,720 --> 00:19:47,760
meaning breaking radiation.

523
00:19:45,360 --> 00:19:50,080
Here's what happens. A primary electron

524
00:19:47,760 --> 00:19:51,679
beam accelerated toward the target

525
00:19:50,080 --> 00:19:54,000
passes close to the nucleus of a

526
00:19:51,679 --> 00:19:56,240
tungsten atom. Because the nucleus is

527
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:57,840
positively charged and the electron is

528
00:19:56,240 --> 00:20:00,240
negatively charged, there is an

529
00:19:57,840 --> 00:20:02,480
attractive force. This force bends the

530
00:20:00,240 --> 00:20:04,080
trajectory of the electron.

531
00:20:02,480 --> 00:20:06,720
Whenever a charged particle is

532
00:20:04,080 --> 00:20:08,960
accelerated or decelerated, it gives off

533
00:20:06,720 --> 00:20:11,200
radiation. In this case, that radiation

534
00:20:08,960 --> 00:20:13,679
is X-rays. Since the amount of bending

535
00:20:11,200 --> 00:20:15,919
can vary, the emitted X-rays span a

536
00:20:13,679 --> 00:20:18,240
continuous range of energies. That's why

537
00:20:15,919 --> 00:20:19,840
bremstral lung is also called continuous

538
00:20:18,240 --> 00:20:21,360
radiation.

539
00:20:19,840 --> 00:20:23,440
The second mechanism is called

540
00:20:21,360 --> 00:20:25,360
characteristic radiation. Sometimes

541
00:20:23,440 --> 00:20:27,760
explained in terms of the photoelectric

542
00:20:25,360 --> 00:20:30,000
effect. In this case, a high energy

543
00:20:27,760 --> 00:20:32,240
primary electron collides with an inner

544
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:34,400
shell electron of the tungsten atom and

545
00:20:32,240 --> 00:20:36,320
kicks it out of its orbit. That leaves a

546
00:20:34,400 --> 00:20:38,240
vacancy in the inner shell. An outer

547
00:20:36,320 --> 00:20:39,919
shell electron then drops down to fill

548
00:20:38,240 --> 00:20:41,600
the gap.

549
00:20:39,919 --> 00:20:43,520
The energy difference between the two

550
00:20:41,600 --> 00:20:46,159
shells is released in the form of an

551
00:20:43,520 --> 00:20:48,080
X-ray photon. Because the energy levels

552
00:20:46,159 --> 00:20:50,400
of atomic shells are discreet, the

553
00:20:48,080 --> 00:20:52,799
emitted X-ray has a very specific

554
00:20:50,400 --> 00:20:55,440
energy, a characteristic value that

555
00:20:52,799 --> 00:20:58,720
depends on the target material.

556
00:20:55,440 --> 00:21:01,360
So to summarize, brester lung gives us a

557
00:20:58,720 --> 00:21:03,600
broad continuous X-ray spectrum while

558
00:21:01,360 --> 00:21:06,240
characteristic radiation adds sharp

559
00:21:03,600 --> 00:21:08,720
peaks at specific energies. Together,

560
00:21:06,240 --> 00:21:10,640
these two processes explain how an X-ray

561
00:21:08,720 --> 00:21:14,520
tube produces the mixture of radiation

562
00:21:10,640 --> 00:21:14,520
that we use for imaging.

563
00:21:15,760 --> 00:21:19,280
Now that we've discussed how X-rays are

564
00:21:17,520 --> 00:21:21,039
generated, let's look at the spectrum

565
00:21:19,280 --> 00:21:23,120
they produce.

566
00:21:21,039 --> 00:21:25,039
First, notice the broad continuous

567
00:21:23,120 --> 00:21:27,440
curve. This comes from Bremstro

568
00:21:25,039 --> 00:21:29,440
radiation. You can see that at lower

569
00:21:27,440 --> 00:21:31,440
photon energies, the intensity is

570
00:21:29,440 --> 00:21:33,919
relatively high and as the energy

571
00:21:31,440 --> 00:21:36,080
increases, the intensity decreases.

572
00:21:33,919 --> 00:21:37,760
However, the very lowest energy X-rays

573
00:21:36,080 --> 00:21:39,280
don't actually appear in the output

574
00:21:37,760 --> 00:21:41,919
because they are absorbed inside the

575
00:21:39,280 --> 00:21:43,360
tungsten target itself. Since the

576
00:21:41,919 --> 00:21:45,440
radiation is produced beneath the

577
00:21:43,360 --> 00:21:47,440
surface, the tungsten material acts as a

578
00:21:45,440 --> 00:21:50,960
natural filter, removing the very soft

579
00:21:47,440 --> 00:21:53,679
X-rays before they can escape.

580
00:21:50,960 --> 00:21:55,679
Next, at higher energies around 70 kiler

581
00:21:53,679 --> 00:21:57,679
volts and above for tungsten, you start

582
00:21:55,679 --> 00:22:00,000
to see sharp peaks. These are the

583
00:21:57,679 --> 00:22:02,480
characteristic X-rays labeled here as K

584
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:04,559
alpha and K beta.

585
00:22:02,480 --> 00:22:06,799
They occur only when the incoming

586
00:22:04,559 --> 00:22:09,039
electron energy is high enough to knock

587
00:22:06,799 --> 00:22:10,720
out inner shell electrons, allowing

588
00:22:09,039 --> 00:22:12,960
outer electrons to fall into those

589
00:22:10,720 --> 00:22:15,039
vacancies and release very specific

590
00:22:12,960 --> 00:22:17,280
amounts of energy.

591
00:22:15,039 --> 00:22:19,520
The maximum X-ray energy you can obtain

592
00:22:17,280 --> 00:22:22,240
is determined by the tube voltage. For

593
00:22:19,520 --> 00:22:24,799
example, if the tube is operated at 120

594
00:22:22,240 --> 00:22:28,080
kovts, then the maximum possible photon

595
00:22:24,799 --> 00:22:30,799
energy is 120 kilo electron volts.

596
00:22:28,080 --> 00:22:32,640
However, those very high energy photons

597
00:22:30,799 --> 00:22:35,280
are less common compared to the middle

598
00:22:32,640 --> 00:22:37,679
range.

599
00:22:35,280 --> 00:22:39,440
Finally, the intensity of the spectrum,

600
00:22:37,679 --> 00:22:41,600
meaning the number of X-ray photons

601
00:22:39,440 --> 00:22:43,440
produced, depends strongly on the tube

602
00:22:41,600 --> 00:22:45,280
current. A higher current means more

603
00:22:43,440 --> 00:22:47,520
electrons striking the target, which

604
00:22:45,280 --> 00:22:49,039
produces more X-ray photons across the

605
00:22:47,520 --> 00:22:50,880
spectrum.

606
00:22:49,039 --> 00:22:53,200
So, the shape of the X-ray spectrum is

607
00:22:50,880 --> 00:22:54,960
governed by three key factors. the

608
00:22:53,200 --> 00:22:56,960
filtering effect of the target, the

609
00:22:54,960 --> 00:22:58,640
discrete characteristic peaks, and the

610
00:22:56,960 --> 00:23:00,159
limits set by the tube voltage and

611
00:22:58,640 --> 00:23:01,919
current.

612
00:23:00,159 --> 00:23:03,200
And keep in mind, this will also connect

613
00:23:01,919 --> 00:23:05,039
directly to one of your homework

614
00:23:03,200 --> 00:23:08,039
problems, so be sure to review it

615
00:23:05,039 --> 00:23:08,039
carefully.

616
00:23:09,760 --> 00:23:13,520
This slide taken from your textbook

617
00:23:11,840 --> 00:23:16,000
shows some important parameters of the

618
00:23:13,520 --> 00:23:17,679
X-ray tube. Let's start with the

619
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:20,080
cathode.

620
00:23:17,679 --> 00:23:22,480
In practice, the cathode is often shaped

621
00:23:20,080 --> 00:23:24,480
in a particular way and charged so that

622
00:23:22,480 --> 00:23:26,720
it helps to focus the electron beam.

623
00:23:24,480 --> 00:23:28,960
Sometimes additional focusing coils are

624
00:23:26,720 --> 00:23:31,200
also placed in the middle region. The

625
00:23:28,960 --> 00:23:33,200
purpose is always the same to narrow the

626
00:23:31,200 --> 00:23:35,440
electron beam and concentrate it onto a

627
00:23:33,200 --> 00:23:38,799
small area of the anode. That small

628
00:23:35,440 --> 00:23:42,960
region becomes the X-ray focal spot. Why

629
00:23:38,799 --> 00:23:44,960
is the focal spot size important?

630
00:23:42,960 --> 00:23:46,799
If the spot is too large, X-rays are

631
00:23:44,960 --> 00:23:48,960
emitted from a broad area and the

632
00:23:46,799 --> 00:23:51,440
resulting image becomes blurry, similar

633
00:23:48,960 --> 00:23:53,520
to motion blur in photography. A smaller

634
00:23:51,440 --> 00:23:56,000
focal spot, on the other hand, produces

635
00:23:53,520 --> 00:23:58,000
much sharper images.

636
00:23:56,000 --> 00:24:00,240
There's also a clever design trick here

637
00:23:58,000 --> 00:24:02,640
called the line focus principle. The

638
00:24:00,240 --> 00:24:04,799
anode surface is angled at some angle

639
00:24:02,640 --> 00:24:07,360
theta. The electrons strike the slope

640
00:24:04,799 --> 00:24:09,280
surface and X-rays are emitted. Because

641
00:24:07,360 --> 00:24:11,520
of the geometry, the apparent or

642
00:24:09,280 --> 00:24:15,039
effective focal spot size is equal to

643
00:24:11,520 --> 00:24:16,880
the actual size times the sign of theta.

644
00:24:15,039 --> 00:24:18,559
This way, you can achieve a smaller

645
00:24:16,880 --> 00:24:20,000
effective focal spot while still

646
00:24:18,559 --> 00:24:22,240
spreading the heat over a larger

647
00:24:20,000 --> 00:24:24,159
physical area. It's a neat compromise

648
00:24:22,240 --> 00:24:25,919
between sharp imaging and thermal

649
00:24:24,159 --> 00:24:28,480
management.

650
00:24:25,919 --> 00:24:30,080
Finally, the field of view or coverage

651
00:24:28,480 --> 00:24:32,240
depends on the distance from the focal

652
00:24:30,080 --> 00:24:34,880
spot to the patient. As the patient

653
00:24:32,240 --> 00:24:36,960
moves farther away, the coverage widens.

654
00:24:34,880 --> 00:24:39,120
The maximum coverage is limited by the

655
00:24:36,960 --> 00:24:41,760
anode angle.

656
00:24:39,120 --> 00:24:44,080
So to summarize, focusing gives us a

657
00:24:41,760 --> 00:24:46,080
sharp spot. The angled anode helps

658
00:24:44,080 --> 00:24:48,080
reduce blur while managing heat. And the

659
00:24:46,080 --> 00:24:52,200
geometry of the tube determines how wide

660
00:24:48,080 --> 00:24:52,200
a field of view we can capture.

661
00:24:53,520 --> 00:24:57,520
We've now reviewed the conventional

662
00:24:55,200 --> 00:24:59,919
X-ray tube, its parameters, and how it

663
00:24:57,520 --> 00:25:02,240
works. Let me briefly mention a new type

664
00:24:59,919 --> 00:25:05,520
of X-ray tube, which represents a more

665
00:25:02,240 --> 00:25:08,000
advanced and expensive technology.

666
00:25:05,520 --> 00:25:10,480
Instead of using a solid tungsten anode,

667
00:25:08,000 --> 00:25:12,240
this design uses a liquid metal anode. A

668
00:25:10,480 --> 00:25:15,039
thin jet of liquid metal flows

669
00:25:12,240 --> 00:25:16,799
continuously downward. An electron beam

670
00:25:15,039 --> 00:25:18,640
generated in a way similar to what we

671
00:25:16,799 --> 00:25:20,559
discussed before strikes the liquid

672
00:25:18,640 --> 00:25:22,559
metal stream. The interaction between

673
00:25:20,559 --> 00:25:24,799
the electrons and the liquid target

674
00:25:22,559 --> 00:25:26,799
produces X-rays.

675
00:25:24,799 --> 00:25:28,799
The advantage of this design is that the

676
00:25:26,799 --> 00:25:30,960
liquid metal can absorb and dissipate

677
00:25:28,799 --> 00:25:32,960
heat much more effectively than a solid

678
00:25:30,960 --> 00:25:35,039
target. As a result, the system can

679
00:25:32,960 --> 00:25:37,520
generate a much brighter X-ray beam,

680
00:25:35,039 --> 00:25:39,919
higher intensity and higher flux without

681
00:25:37,520 --> 00:25:42,320
destroying the anode.

682
00:25:39,919 --> 00:25:43,840
These tubes are very costly, often

683
00:25:42,320 --> 00:25:45,840
priced in the range of hundreds of

684
00:25:43,840 --> 00:25:47,520
thousands of dollars. We won't test you

685
00:25:45,840 --> 00:25:49,600
on the details of this system, but it's

686
00:25:47,520 --> 00:25:51,600
good to be aware of such innovations.

687
00:25:49,600 --> 00:25:54,240
They illustrate how researchers continue

688
00:25:51,600 --> 00:25:56,159
to push the limits of X-ray technology,

689
00:25:54,240 --> 00:25:57,919
refining designs to achieve better

690
00:25:56,159 --> 00:25:59,760
performance.

691
00:25:57,919 --> 00:26:02,080
I should also add a deeper point for you

692
00:25:59,760 --> 00:26:04,400
to think about. When we describe BMRong

693
00:26:02,080 --> 00:26:06,480
radiation, it may sound puzzling.

694
00:26:04,400 --> 00:26:08,720
Electrons are attracted to the nucleus.

695
00:26:06,480 --> 00:26:11,120
So at one stage they seem to gain energy

696
00:26:08,720 --> 00:26:13,279
while at another stage they lose energy.

697
00:26:11,120 --> 00:26:17,039
How can net radiation be produced if

698
00:26:13,279 --> 00:26:18,960
these processes are symmetric?

699
00:26:17,039 --> 00:26:21,679
The key is that the trajectory of the

700
00:26:18,960 --> 00:26:23,440
electron is bent. That bending means the

701
00:26:21,679 --> 00:26:25,440
electron is accelerated in a new

702
00:26:23,440 --> 00:26:27,919
direction. An acceleration of charge

703
00:26:25,440 --> 00:26:29,840
always produces radiation. This is the

704
00:26:27,919 --> 00:26:31,760
fundamental physical basis conservation

705
00:26:29,840 --> 00:26:34,640
laws and electromagnetic theory together

706
00:26:31,760 --> 00:26:36,640
explain why X-rays are emitted.

707
00:26:34,640 --> 00:26:38,240
So while the equations provide a clear

708
00:26:36,640 --> 00:26:41,279
description, it's important to connect

709
00:26:38,240 --> 00:26:42,960
them with the underlying physics.

710
00:26:41,279 --> 00:26:45,039
That's what allows us to understand not

711
00:26:42,960 --> 00:26:47,200
just how X-rays are generated, but also

712
00:26:45,039 --> 00:26:49,200
how new designs like this liquid metal

713
00:26:47,200 --> 00:26:53,240
tube can open the door to brighter and

714
00:26:49,200 --> 00:26:53,240
more efficient X-ray sources.

715
00:26:54,640 --> 00:26:58,799
By now you understand how X-rays are

716
00:26:56,799 --> 00:27:00,960
generated. That knowledge positions you

717
00:26:58,799 --> 00:27:02,720
well not only for deeper research in

718
00:27:00,960 --> 00:27:05,919
this field but also for practical

719
00:27:02,720 --> 00:27:07,760
discussions even in job interviews.

720
00:27:05,919 --> 00:27:10,159
Now we move to the next important part

721
00:27:07,760 --> 00:27:12,559
of the imaging chain. X-ray attenuation.

722
00:27:10,159 --> 00:27:14,720
Once X-rays are produced by the tube or

723
00:27:12,559 --> 00:27:17,039
in some cases by a synretron radiation

724
00:27:14,720 --> 00:27:20,240
source, we use them to probe an object

725
00:27:17,039 --> 00:27:21,679
or a patient. As the X-rays travel

726
00:27:20,240 --> 00:27:23,760
through matter, some of them are

727
00:27:21,679 --> 00:27:26,480
absorbed, some are scattered, and some

728
00:27:23,760 --> 00:27:28,559
pass through with little interaction.

729
00:27:26,480 --> 00:27:30,240
It's in this process, the way X-rays are

730
00:27:28,559 --> 00:27:32,400
attenuated by different tissues or

731
00:27:30,240 --> 00:27:34,720
materials that valuable diagnostic

732
00:27:32,400 --> 00:27:36,960
information is encoded. Understanding

733
00:27:34,720 --> 00:27:38,799
attenuation is crucial because it

734
00:27:36,960 --> 00:27:43,240
directly determines the contrast and

735
00:27:38,799 --> 00:27:43,240
quality of the images we can produce.

736
00:27:44,799 --> 00:27:49,880
So how exactly do X-rays get attenuated?

737
00:27:50,159 --> 00:27:54,320
Think about it this way. When an X-ray

738
00:27:52,320 --> 00:27:56,640
beam passes through the body, several

739
00:27:54,320 --> 00:27:58,880
outcomes are possible. If there is no

740
00:27:56,640 --> 00:28:00,480
interaction at all, the X-rays travel

741
00:27:58,880 --> 00:28:02,320
straight through as if the body were

742
00:28:00,480 --> 00:28:04,320
completely transparent. That doesn't

743
00:28:02,320 --> 00:28:05,840
help us because no useful information is

744
00:28:04,320 --> 00:28:08,080
recorded.

745
00:28:05,840 --> 00:28:10,159
At the other extreme, if there is total

746
00:28:08,080 --> 00:28:13,120
absorption, none of the X-rays make it

747
00:28:10,159 --> 00:28:15,039
out. That also gives us no signal. The

748
00:28:13,120 --> 00:28:16,640
most useful case is partial absorption,

749
00:28:15,039 --> 00:28:18,480
and that occurs through two main

750
00:28:16,640 --> 00:28:20,480
processes, scattering and the

751
00:28:18,480 --> 00:28:25,039
photoelectric effect. We'll talk about

752
00:28:20,480 --> 00:28:27,520
these in more detail on the next slides.

753
00:28:25,039 --> 00:28:30,000
Now, at the atomic level, here's what is

754
00:28:27,520 --> 00:28:32,080
happening. X-rays are photons, tiny

755
00:28:30,000 --> 00:28:34,720
packets of energy. When they strike the

756
00:28:32,080 --> 00:28:37,919
body, they may interact with molecules,

757
00:28:34,720 --> 00:28:40,080
atoms, or even inner electrons.

758
00:28:37,919 --> 00:28:42,240
Some photons pass through unchanged,

759
00:28:40,080 --> 00:28:44,240
forming the primary beam. Others are

760
00:28:42,240 --> 00:28:46,320
absorbed completely, transferring all

761
00:28:44,240 --> 00:28:48,480
their energy to the material, and still

762
00:28:46,320 --> 00:28:50,720
others are scattered, deflected in new

763
00:28:48,480 --> 00:28:53,360
directions, sometimes losing energy in

764
00:28:50,720 --> 00:28:55,039
the process. It's the balance of these

765
00:28:53,360 --> 00:28:57,279
interactions that produces image

766
00:28:55,039 --> 00:29:01,360
contrast. Different tissues in the body,

767
00:28:57,279 --> 00:29:03,279
bone, muscle, fat, lung attenuate X-rays

768
00:29:01,360 --> 00:29:07,720
to different degrees, which is why we

769
00:29:03,279 --> 00:29:07,720
can see structures in an X-ray image.

770
00:29:09,039 --> 00:29:12,880
Here we see the overall picture of how

771
00:29:10,720 --> 00:29:14,640
X-ray photons interact with matter. At

772
00:29:12,880 --> 00:29:17,679
the atomic level, these interactions can

773
00:29:14,640 --> 00:29:20,240
be grouped into four main categories.

774
00:29:17,679 --> 00:29:22,000
The first case is no interaction. The

775
00:29:20,240 --> 00:29:24,000
photon simply passes through the

776
00:29:22,000 --> 00:29:26,080
material unchanged. You could say the

777
00:29:24,000 --> 00:29:28,640
photon gets lucky. It doesn't collide

778
00:29:26,080 --> 00:29:30,640
with anything. That photon contributes

779
00:29:28,640 --> 00:29:33,440
to the primary beam that reaches the

780
00:29:30,640 --> 00:29:35,120
detector. The second case is total

781
00:29:33,440 --> 00:29:37,919
absorption also known as the

782
00:29:35,120 --> 00:29:39,760
photoelectric effect. Here the photon is

783
00:29:37,919 --> 00:29:41,760
completely absorbed by an atom. It

784
00:29:39,760 --> 00:29:43,600
transfers all its energy to an inner

785
00:29:41,760 --> 00:29:45,679
electron which is ejected as a

786
00:29:43,600 --> 00:29:47,760
photoeleron. The atom is left with a

787
00:29:45,679 --> 00:29:49,440
vacancy and when electrons from higher

788
00:29:47,760 --> 00:29:52,480
shells fall into that vacancy

789
00:29:49,440 --> 00:29:54,640
characteristic x-rays can be emitted.

790
00:29:52,480 --> 00:29:57,039
The other two cases involve scattering.

791
00:29:54,640 --> 00:29:58,799
Incoherent or really scattering, the

792
00:29:57,039 --> 00:30:01,120
photon is deflected at a very small

793
00:29:58,799 --> 00:30:03,200
angle but its energy remains the same

794
00:30:01,120 --> 00:30:05,600
because the frequency and phase of the

795
00:30:03,200 --> 00:30:08,159
photon do not change. We call this

796
00:30:05,600 --> 00:30:10,720
coherent scattering.

797
00:30:08,159 --> 00:30:12,640
In contrast, incoherent scattering, also

798
00:30:10,720 --> 00:30:14,640
called Compton scattering, happens when

799
00:30:12,640 --> 00:30:16,399
the photon collides with an outer shell

800
00:30:14,640 --> 00:30:18,399
electron and transfers part of its

801
00:30:16,399 --> 00:30:20,399
energy to it. The electron is ejected

802
00:30:18,399 --> 00:30:23,039
and the photon is scattered at a larger

803
00:30:20,399 --> 00:30:24,960
angle with reduced energy. The energy

804
00:30:23,039 --> 00:30:26,799
lost by the photon is taken up by the

805
00:30:24,960 --> 00:30:30,159
electron and eventually dissipates as

806
00:30:26,799 --> 00:30:31,520
heat. For medical imaging, the two most

807
00:30:30,159 --> 00:30:33,520
important interactions are the

808
00:30:31,520 --> 00:30:35,440
photoelectric effect and Compton

809
00:30:33,520 --> 00:30:37,840
scattering. The photoelectric effect

810
00:30:35,440 --> 00:30:39,760
gives rise to absorption contrast while

811
00:30:37,840 --> 00:30:41,919
Compton scattering dominates at higher

812
00:30:39,760 --> 00:30:44,720
energies and contributes to image noise

813
00:30:41,919 --> 00:30:47,919
and radiation dose.

814
00:30:44,720 --> 00:30:49,919
So to summarize, no interaction, total

815
00:30:47,919 --> 00:30:51,760
absorption, coherent scattering, and

816
00:30:49,919 --> 00:30:53,600
Compton scattering. These four

817
00:30:51,760 --> 00:30:57,640
categories describe the essential ways

818
00:30:53,600 --> 00:30:57,640
X-rays interact with tissue.

819
00:30:59,120 --> 00:31:03,200
Here is a summary of the different types

820
00:31:00,960 --> 00:31:04,960
of X-ray interactions with matter. For

821
00:31:03,200 --> 00:31:07,440
medical X-rays, the two dominant

822
00:31:04,960 --> 00:31:09,840
processes are the photoelectric effect

823
00:31:07,440 --> 00:31:11,760
and Compton scattering.

824
00:31:09,840 --> 00:31:14,320
Scattering can be divided into Compton

825
00:31:11,760 --> 00:31:15,919
and coherent or RA scattering. But in

826
00:31:14,320 --> 00:31:17,919
practice, coherent scattering

827
00:31:15,919 --> 00:31:19,679
contributes only a very small portion

828
00:31:17,919 --> 00:31:22,720
and is usually not considered important

829
00:31:19,679 --> 00:31:25,039
for medical imaging.

830
00:31:22,720 --> 00:31:26,960
Now in clinical X-ray systems, we

831
00:31:25,039 --> 00:31:31,200
typically operate with tube voltages up

832
00:31:26,960 --> 00:31:33,840
to about 140 kovts peak or KVP.

833
00:31:31,200 --> 00:31:36,159
When the energy is below about 80 kVP,

834
00:31:33,840 --> 00:31:39,600
the photoelectric effect is the dominant

835
00:31:36,159 --> 00:31:41,519
interaction. Above 80 kVp, Compton

836
00:31:39,600 --> 00:31:43,919
scattering becomes more significant.

837
00:31:41,519 --> 00:31:45,360
This is why in diagnostic imaging, we

838
00:31:43,919 --> 00:31:48,240
see a transition in the relative

839
00:31:45,360 --> 00:31:50,000
contributions of these two effects.

840
00:31:48,240 --> 00:31:51,840
Traditionally, scattering was treated

841
00:31:50,000 --> 00:31:54,159
mainly as a nuisance because it reduces

842
00:31:51,840 --> 00:31:55,840
image contrast. But modern research

843
00:31:54,159 --> 00:31:57,919
shows that small angle Compton

844
00:31:55,840 --> 00:31:59,600
scattering actually carries valuable

845
00:31:57,919 --> 00:32:01,360
information.

846
00:31:59,600 --> 00:32:03,200
In fact, subtle differences in

847
00:32:01,360 --> 00:32:05,679
scattering patterns may help distinguish

848
00:32:03,200 --> 00:32:07,760
benign from malignant tumors. However,

849
00:32:05,679 --> 00:32:10,000
detecting and analyzing this information

850
00:32:07,760 --> 00:32:12,720
requires specialized techniques which go

851
00:32:10,000 --> 00:32:14,480
beyond the scope of our class. In

852
00:32:12,720 --> 00:32:16,799
addition to these, there are two higher

853
00:32:14,480 --> 00:32:19,200
energy interactions, pair production and

854
00:32:16,799 --> 00:32:21,120
photo disintegration. These processes

855
00:32:19,200 --> 00:32:23,200
become relevant only when the photon

856
00:32:21,120 --> 00:32:25,600
energy is very high, well above the

857
00:32:23,200 --> 00:32:27,679
diagnostic range.

858
00:32:25,600 --> 00:32:29,840
For example, in radiation therapy where

859
00:32:27,679 --> 00:32:31,919
high energy X-rays or proton beams are

860
00:32:29,840 --> 00:32:33,600
directed at tumors, pair production and

861
00:32:31,919 --> 00:32:36,080
photo disintegration play important

862
00:32:33,600 --> 00:32:38,559
roles. In that context, the goal is not

863
00:32:36,080 --> 00:32:42,000
diagnosis but treatment. Essentially

864
00:32:38,559 --> 00:32:45,120
using radiation to destroy tumor tissue.

865
00:32:42,000 --> 00:32:47,279
So to summarize in diagnostic radiology

866
00:32:45,120 --> 00:32:49,600
photoelectric absorption and compant

867
00:32:47,279 --> 00:32:51,360
scattering are the key effects for

868
00:32:49,600 --> 00:32:53,600
therapeutic applications at higher

869
00:32:51,360 --> 00:32:58,120
energies pair production and photodice

870
00:32:53,600 --> 00:32:58,120
integration also come into play.

871
00:32:59,679 --> 00:33:04,080
Compton scattering has major

872
00:33:01,120 --> 00:33:06,399
implications for radiation protection.

873
00:33:04,080 --> 00:33:08,240
When we use X-rays on a patient most of

874
00:33:06,399 --> 00:33:10,320
the useful photons travel through the

875
00:33:08,240 --> 00:33:12,480
body and reach the detector to form the

876
00:33:10,320 --> 00:33:14,720
image. But many photons do not follow

877
00:33:12,480 --> 00:33:17,120
this direct path. Instead, they are

878
00:33:14,720 --> 00:33:18,960
scattered in different directions. These

879
00:33:17,120 --> 00:33:20,960
scattered photons, especially those from

880
00:33:18,960 --> 00:33:22,640
Compton scattering, can expose people

881
00:33:20,960 --> 00:33:24,559
nearby.

882
00:33:22,640 --> 00:33:26,640
That is why radiation protection is so

883
00:33:24,559 --> 00:33:29,120
critical. For patients, the exposure is

884
00:33:26,640 --> 00:33:30,640
usually occasional, maybe once a year or

885
00:33:29,120 --> 00:33:32,640
only when necessary. But for

886
00:33:30,640 --> 00:33:34,960
radiologists and technicians working in

887
00:33:32,640 --> 00:33:36,720
X-ray rooms every day, the exposure

888
00:33:34,960 --> 00:33:39,519
could accumulate over time if they are

889
00:33:36,720 --> 00:33:41,679
not protected.

890
00:33:39,519 --> 00:33:43,760
To minimize this risk, we use lead

891
00:33:41,679 --> 00:33:46,240
shielding such as lead glass windows and

892
00:33:43,760 --> 00:33:48,320
lead aprons. These barriers absorb

893
00:33:46,240 --> 00:33:50,960
scattered radiation and protect staff

894
00:33:48,320 --> 00:33:52,880
from unnecessary dose. Without them,

895
00:33:50,960 --> 00:33:54,640
repeated daily exposure could become

896
00:33:52,880 --> 00:33:56,640
dangerous.

897
00:33:54,640 --> 00:33:58,640
So, one of the key lessons is that while

898
00:33:56,640 --> 00:34:01,200
X-rays are extremely valuable for

899
00:33:58,640 --> 00:34:03,120
diagnosis, we must always use proper

900
00:34:01,200 --> 00:34:06,519
shielding and safety measures to protect

901
00:34:03,120 --> 00:34:06,519
healthcare workers.

902
00:34:07,840 --> 00:34:11,440
As X-rays pass through biological

903
00:34:09,839 --> 00:34:13,599
tissue, they are progressively

904
00:34:11,440 --> 00:34:16,159
attenuated. This is a cumulative

905
00:34:13,599 --> 00:34:19,679
process. The number of photons decreases

906
00:34:16,159 --> 00:34:21,119
rapidly as the beam penetrates deeper.

907
00:34:19,679 --> 00:34:23,359
You can think of the tissue as being

908
00:34:21,119 --> 00:34:24,800
made up of thin layers. Each layer

909
00:34:23,359 --> 00:34:26,879
absorbs a certain fraction of the

910
00:34:24,800 --> 00:34:28,800
incoming photons. A useful way to

911
00:34:26,879 --> 00:34:31,679
describe this is the half value layer

912
00:34:28,800 --> 00:34:33,839
concept. Suppose you start with 1,000

913
00:34:31,679 --> 00:34:35,280
X-ray photons.

914
00:34:33,839 --> 00:34:37,679
After passing through the first half

915
00:34:35,280 --> 00:34:40,480
value layer, about half remain, 500

916
00:34:37,679 --> 00:34:42,480
photons. After a second half value layer

917
00:34:40,480 --> 00:34:45,839
of the same thickness, the number drops

918
00:34:42,480 --> 00:34:47,679
to about 250. After a few layers, only a

919
00:34:45,839 --> 00:34:50,639
small fraction of the original photon

920
00:34:47,679 --> 00:34:52,720
survive.

921
00:34:50,639 --> 00:34:54,720
So attenuation is really a matter of

922
00:34:52,720 --> 00:34:56,800
probability. Each layer has the same

923
00:34:54,720 --> 00:34:58,640
chance of absorbing a photon and the

924
00:34:56,800 --> 00:35:00,079
result is an exponential reduction in

925
00:34:58,640 --> 00:35:01,680
the beam.

926
00:35:00,079 --> 00:35:04,079
It's also worth noting that X-ray

927
00:35:01,680 --> 00:35:06,480
imaging is not very efficient overall.

928
00:35:04,079 --> 00:35:08,800
In the tube, only about 1% of the

929
00:35:06,480 --> 00:35:12,800
electron energy is converted into usable

930
00:35:08,800 --> 00:35:15,200
X-ray photons. The rest is lost as heat.

931
00:35:12,800 --> 00:35:17,359
Then after filtration and attenuation

932
00:35:15,200 --> 00:35:19,280
inside the patient, only a small

933
00:35:17,359 --> 00:35:21,760
percentage of the original photons

934
00:35:19,280 --> 00:35:23,760
actually reach the detector.

935
00:35:21,760 --> 00:35:25,440
That's why we need two things. smart

936
00:35:23,760 --> 00:35:27,119
algorithms to make the most of the

937
00:35:25,440 --> 00:35:29,200
limited data and highly sensitive

938
00:35:27,119 --> 00:35:32,079
detectors to record even the relatively

939
00:35:29,200 --> 00:35:34,480
small number of photons that emerge.

940
00:35:32,079 --> 00:35:36,480
So now you have the physical intuition.

941
00:35:34,480 --> 00:35:38,960
Next we will introduce the mathematical

942
00:35:36,480 --> 00:35:40,960
description of attenuation Beer's law

943
00:35:38,960 --> 00:35:44,599
which provides a precise model for this

944
00:35:40,960 --> 00:35:44,599
exponential process.

945
00:35:46,240 --> 00:35:52,320
Now let's describe X-ray attenuation in

946
00:35:48,720 --> 00:35:54,800
a more quantitative way. Imagine a beam

947
00:35:52,320 --> 00:35:57,040
of X-ray photons entering a small region

948
00:35:54,800 --> 00:35:59,839
of tissue. You can think of this region

949
00:35:57,040 --> 00:36:02,000
as a pixel, a tiny picture element made

950
00:35:59,839 --> 00:36:04,320
of uniform material. The number of

951
00:36:02,000 --> 00:36:06,480
photons that enter is what we call the

952
00:36:04,320 --> 00:36:08,160
input intensity. The number that comes

953
00:36:06,480 --> 00:36:09,760
out on the other side is the output

954
00:36:08,160 --> 00:36:11,599
intensity.

955
00:36:09,760 --> 00:36:14,079
The relationship between the input and

956
00:36:11,599 --> 00:36:16,079
output depends on two key factors.

957
00:36:14,079 --> 00:36:17,760
First, the property of the material

958
00:36:16,079 --> 00:36:20,000
which we describe using the linear

959
00:36:17,760 --> 00:36:22,560
attenuation coefficient written as the

960
00:36:20,000 --> 00:36:25,760
Greek letter mu. Second, the thickness

961
00:36:22,560 --> 00:36:27,440
of the material which we call delta x.

962
00:36:25,760 --> 00:36:29,839
Putting this together, the output

963
00:36:27,440 --> 00:36:32,240
intensity equals the input intensity

964
00:36:29,839 --> 00:36:34,480
multiplied by an exponential decay term.

965
00:36:32,240 --> 00:36:36,079
In plain language, this means that as

966
00:36:34,480 --> 00:36:38,560
the x-ray beam passes through the

967
00:36:36,079 --> 00:36:42,079
tissue, the number of photons drops off

968
00:36:38,560 --> 00:36:44,240
exponentially with thickness.

969
00:36:42,079 --> 00:36:46,000
This exponential law comes directly from

970
00:36:44,240 --> 00:36:48,320
solving a first order differential

971
00:36:46,000 --> 00:36:50,800
equation. The idea is simple. If you

972
00:36:48,320 --> 00:36:53,040
look at a very thin layer, only a small

973
00:36:50,800 --> 00:36:55,280
fraction of the photons are absorbed

974
00:36:53,040 --> 00:36:56,960
proportional to the thickness. Stack up

975
00:36:55,280 --> 00:36:59,760
many thin layers and the result

976
00:36:56,960 --> 00:37:02,160
naturally becomes exponential.

977
00:36:59,760 --> 00:37:04,800
In fact, this same mathematical behavior

978
00:37:02,160 --> 00:37:08,079
appears in many other fields. Chemical

979
00:37:04,800 --> 00:37:10,720
reactions, radioactive decay, and even

980
00:37:08,079 --> 00:37:12,800
population growth or decline.

981
00:37:10,720 --> 00:37:14,960
Now in the human body the situation is

982
00:37:12,800 --> 00:37:16,800
more complex because the X-ray beam

983
00:37:14,960 --> 00:37:18,960
usually passes through several tissues

984
00:37:16,800 --> 00:37:20,800
with different properties. In that case

985
00:37:18,960 --> 00:37:22,800
the output is determined by the combined

986
00:37:20,800 --> 00:37:26,160
attenuation from all of them along the

987
00:37:22,800 --> 00:37:28,720
path. This exponential attenuation law

988
00:37:26,160 --> 00:37:30,960
is fundamental to X-ray imaging. It is

989
00:37:28,720 --> 00:37:33,839
the mathematical backbone of both

990
00:37:30,960 --> 00:37:36,160
radiography and CT because it precisely

991
00:37:33,839 --> 00:37:40,200
tells us how X-ray photons are reduced

992
00:37:36,160 --> 00:37:40,200
as they traverse the body.

993
00:37:41,359 --> 00:37:45,040
Now let's extend the exponential

994
00:37:43,040 --> 00:37:46,880
attenuation model to the case where the

995
00:37:45,040 --> 00:37:49,520
X-ray beam passes through multiple

996
00:37:46,880 --> 00:37:51,599
elements along its path.

997
00:37:49,520 --> 00:37:53,760
Suppose the beam is divided into many

998
00:37:51,599 --> 00:37:55,680
small steps. For each step, there is a

999
00:37:53,760 --> 00:37:58,000
local attenuation coefficient and a

1000
00:37:55,680 --> 00:38:00,720
thickness. When we add up all of these

1001
00:37:58,000 --> 00:38:02,640
contributions, we arrive at a cumulative

1002
00:38:00,720 --> 00:38:04,800
relationship.

1003
00:38:02,640 --> 00:38:07,119
This summation has a special name. We

1004
00:38:04,800 --> 00:38:09,280
call it array sum. The term simply means

1005
00:38:07,119 --> 00:38:11,359
we are summing up contributions along a

1006
00:38:09,280 --> 00:38:13,440
single ray of X-rays as it travels

1007
00:38:11,359 --> 00:38:15,599
through the object.

1008
00:38:13,440 --> 00:38:17,599
Now notice what is known and what is

1009
00:38:15,599 --> 00:38:19,520
unknown. The thickness of each element

1010
00:38:17,599 --> 00:38:21,200
is something we set in the model. What

1011
00:38:19,520 --> 00:38:23,520
we don't know are the attenuation

1012
00:38:21,200 --> 00:38:26,400
coefficients themselves. Those are the

1013
00:38:23,520 --> 00:38:28,560
values we are trying to determine.

1014
00:38:26,400 --> 00:38:31,040
So what we really have is a linear

1015
00:38:28,560 --> 00:38:34,160
combination of unknowns. That's why this

1016
00:38:31,040 --> 00:38:35,839
is described as a linear system. On the

1017
00:38:34,160 --> 00:38:38,240
other side of the equation, you see a

1018
00:38:35,839 --> 00:38:40,240
logarithm. Taking the natural logarithm

1019
00:38:38,240 --> 00:38:42,880
is just a mathematical way of undoing

1020
00:38:40,240 --> 00:38:44,880
the exponential law we started with.

1021
00:38:42,880 --> 00:38:47,280
This step allows us to link the unknown

1022
00:38:44,880 --> 00:38:50,640
attenuation values inside the body with

1023
00:38:47,280 --> 00:38:52,640
quantities we can actually measure.

1024
00:38:50,640 --> 00:38:54,720
Think about it. The input intensity is

1025
00:38:52,640 --> 00:38:57,280
known because we generate and calibrate

1026
00:38:54,720 --> 00:38:59,599
the X-ray beam. The output intensity is

1027
00:38:57,280 --> 00:39:01,440
measured directly by the detector. The

1028
00:38:59,599 --> 00:39:03,680
ratio of the two gives us a measurable

1029
00:39:01,440 --> 00:39:05,520
value. and taking the logarithm turns it

1030
00:39:03,680 --> 00:39:08,560
into the sum of attenuation along the

1031
00:39:05,520 --> 00:39:11,040
path. If we make the elements smaller

1032
00:39:08,560 --> 00:39:13,280
and smaller, the discrete sum becomes a

1033
00:39:11,040 --> 00:39:15,359
continuous integral. In other words, in

1034
00:39:13,280 --> 00:39:17,599
the limit, what we measure is a line

1035
00:39:15,359 --> 00:39:20,160
integral of the attenuation coefficient

1036
00:39:17,599 --> 00:39:22,720
along the path of the X-ray.

1037
00:39:20,160 --> 00:39:24,320
This is the key principle with X-rays.

1038
00:39:22,720 --> 00:39:26,160
We do not directly measure the

1039
00:39:24,320 --> 00:39:28,880
attenuation at each point inside the

1040
00:39:26,160 --> 00:39:30,800
body. Instead, we measure ray sums,

1041
00:39:28,880 --> 00:39:32,800
overlapping signals that represent line

1042
00:39:30,800 --> 00:39:34,560
integrals. By collecting many such

1043
00:39:32,800 --> 00:39:36,240
measurements from different angles, we

1044
00:39:34,560 --> 00:39:37,839
can mathematically reconstruct the

1045
00:39:36,240 --> 00:39:39,839
image.

1046
00:39:37,839 --> 00:39:42,000
So, this linear equation forms the

1047
00:39:39,839 --> 00:39:44,240
bridge between the unknown interior of

1048
00:39:42,000 --> 00:39:47,240
the body and the measurable data at the

1049
00:39:44,240 --> 00:39:47,240
detector.

1050
00:39:48,640 --> 00:39:53,680
So far, we've discussed how X-rays are

1051
00:39:50,800 --> 00:39:55,359
attenuated as they pass through tissue.

1052
00:39:53,680 --> 00:39:57,839
Now let's see how this attenuation

1053
00:39:55,359 --> 00:39:59,760
creates contrast in an image. Imagine a

1054
00:39:57,839 --> 00:40:01,680
piece of tissue that is mostly uniform

1055
00:39:59,760 --> 00:40:03,839
but with a small bump or nodule inside

1056
00:40:01,680 --> 00:40:07,440
it. The X-ray beam passing through the

1057
00:40:03,839 --> 00:40:09,520
normal tissue travels a distance x. Its

1058
00:40:07,440 --> 00:40:14,960
signal is attenuated according to the

1059
00:40:09,520 --> 00:40:16,720
exponential law. A= n e ^ of minus mx.

1060
00:40:14,960 --> 00:40:18,880
Now consider the ray that passes through

1061
00:40:16,720 --> 00:40:21,520
the nodule. This ray travels an

1062
00:40:18,880 --> 00:40:25,440
additional thickness z. Its output is

1063
00:40:21,520 --> 00:40:29,119
therefore b= n e to the power of minus m

1064
00:40:25,440 --> 00:40:30,800
x + z dot. The contrast is defined as

1065
00:40:29,119 --> 00:40:32,400
the difference between the normal signal

1066
00:40:30,800 --> 00:40:34,320
and the signal through the nodule

1067
00:40:32,400 --> 00:40:38,160
divided by the normal signal. Doing the

1068
00:40:34,320 --> 00:40:40,240
math we find contrast equals 1 - e ^ of

1069
00:40:38,160 --> 00:40:42,560
minus mz.

1070
00:40:40,240 --> 00:40:45,839
Notice that the contrast depends only on

1071
00:40:42,560 --> 00:40:49,520
m the attenuation coefficient and z the

1072
00:40:45,839 --> 00:40:52,640
extra thickness of the nodule.

1073
00:40:49,520 --> 00:40:55,280
For example, if the nodule is 1 cm thick

1074
00:40:52,640 --> 00:40:58,319
and m equals 1 per cime, then the

1075
00:40:55,280 --> 00:41:01,359
contrast comes out to about 63%. But in

1076
00:40:58,319 --> 00:41:04,720
real soft tissue, M is much smaller,

1077
00:41:01,359 --> 00:41:07,119
closer to 0.2 per cm. In that case, the

1078
00:41:04,720 --> 00:41:09,200
contrast becomes significantly lower,

1079
00:41:07,119 --> 00:41:10,960
making small nodules much harder to

1080
00:41:09,200 --> 00:41:12,720
detect.

1081
00:41:10,960 --> 00:41:14,880
This simple example shows the mechanism

1082
00:41:12,720 --> 00:41:17,040
of attenuation contrast. The difference

1083
00:41:14,880 --> 00:41:19,119
in signal arises because one path is

1084
00:41:17,040 --> 00:41:22,400
slightly longer or passes through tissue

1085
00:41:19,119 --> 00:41:24,800
with different attenuation properties.

1086
00:41:22,400 --> 00:41:26,880
But in soft tissues where M values are

1087
00:41:24,800 --> 00:41:28,880
close together, contrast can be very

1088
00:41:26,880 --> 00:41:31,040
limited. And that is why techniques like

1089
00:41:28,880 --> 00:41:34,920
contrast agents or advanced imaging

1090
00:41:31,040 --> 00:41:34,920
methods are often necessary.

1091
00:41:36,560 --> 00:41:39,760
Now we can summarize the X-ray

1092
00:41:38,079 --> 00:41:41,760
measurement model, which is one of the

1093
00:41:39,760 --> 00:41:43,359
most important concepts. That's why I've

1094
00:41:41,760 --> 00:41:45,119
marked this slide with the largest red

1095
00:41:43,359 --> 00:41:46,720
diamond.

1096
00:41:45,119 --> 00:41:49,359
Let's begin with the monochromatic

1097
00:41:46,720 --> 00:41:51,359
model. Here we assume the X-ray beam

1098
00:41:49,359 --> 00:41:53,520
consists of photons all having the same

1099
00:41:51,359 --> 00:41:56,240
energy. That's an idealization, but it

1100
00:41:53,520 --> 00:41:58,960
helps us understand the basics.

1101
00:41:56,240 --> 00:42:01,920
In this case, the incoming intensity I

1102
00:41:58,960 --> 00:42:05,359
knenuated according to Beer's law. The

1103
00:42:01,920 --> 00:42:07,440
output, which we call IG, is simply I *

1104
00:42:05,359 --> 00:42:09,599
E the power of minus the line integral

1105
00:42:07,440 --> 00:42:11,359
of M along the path. This means the

1106
00:42:09,599 --> 00:42:13,359
measured signal is directly linked to

1107
00:42:11,359 --> 00:42:16,240
the cumulative attenuation along the

1108
00:42:13,359 --> 00:42:18,319
ray. If you know the input I knot and

1109
00:42:16,240 --> 00:42:20,000
measure the output IG, then by taking

1110
00:42:18,319 --> 00:42:22,480
the logarithm, you recover the line

1111
00:42:20,000 --> 00:42:24,319
integral of the attenuation coefficient.

1112
00:42:22,480 --> 00:42:28,000
That's the foundation of reconstruction

1113
00:42:24,319 --> 00:42:30,160
in X-ray imaging and CT. But in reality,

1114
00:42:28,000 --> 00:42:32,319
things are more complicated. An actual

1115
00:42:30,160 --> 00:42:34,640
X-ray tube does not produce a single

1116
00:42:32,319 --> 00:42:37,119
energy. It generates a spectrum of

1117
00:42:34,640 --> 00:42:39,599
energies. The detector in most systems

1118
00:42:37,119 --> 00:42:41,920
today does not separate them. Instead,

1119
00:42:39,599 --> 00:42:45,520
it integrates the total energy deposited

1120
00:42:41,920 --> 00:42:47,280
whether from soft X-rays or hard X-rays.

1121
00:42:45,520 --> 00:42:49,359
That leads us to the poly chromatic

1122
00:42:47,280 --> 00:42:52,240
model. In this case, we must integrate

1123
00:42:49,359 --> 00:42:54,480
over the entire energy spectrum.

1124
00:42:52,240 --> 00:42:56,880
For each energy, attenuation follows the

1125
00:42:54,480 --> 00:42:59,200
exponential law, but the detector sums

1126
00:42:56,880 --> 00:43:01,200
over all energies together. The result

1127
00:42:59,200 --> 00:43:03,280
is still a single number, a grayscale

1128
00:43:01,200 --> 00:43:06,079
intensity, but it is a weighted sum

1129
00:43:03,280 --> 00:43:08,400
across the energy distribution.

1130
00:43:06,079 --> 00:43:10,560
So the monochromatic model is simple and

1131
00:43:08,400 --> 00:43:12,400
idealized, while the poly chromatic

1132
00:43:10,560 --> 00:43:15,119
model describes the true measurement

1133
00:43:12,400 --> 00:43:17,119
process in clinical CT scanners. And

1134
00:43:15,119 --> 00:43:19,119
this distinction is very important. It

1135
00:43:17,119 --> 00:43:23,240
explains why certain artifacts like beam

1136
00:43:19,119 --> 00:43:23,240
hardening occur in practice.

1137
00:43:24,560 --> 00:43:28,800
A very important concept in X-ray

1138
00:43:26,560 --> 00:43:31,200
imaging is the K edge of the linear

1139
00:43:28,800 --> 00:43:33,119
attenuation coefficient.

1140
00:43:31,200 --> 00:43:35,200
Now remember msir attenuation

1141
00:43:33,119 --> 00:43:37,280
coefficient is not a single fixed

1142
00:43:35,200 --> 00:43:39,839
number. It depends strongly on the

1143
00:43:37,280 --> 00:43:42,319
energy of the x-rays. In general as

1144
00:43:39,839 --> 00:43:44,560
energy increases x-rays penetrate more

1145
00:43:42,319 --> 00:43:47,839
easily. So the attenuation coefficient

1146
00:43:44,560 --> 00:43:49,520
gradually decreases.

1147
00:43:47,839 --> 00:43:51,520
But at certain energies something

1148
00:43:49,520 --> 00:43:53,200
special happens. When the X-ray energy

1149
00:43:51,520 --> 00:43:55,680
becomes high enough to knock out inner

1150
00:43:53,200 --> 00:43:57,599
shell electrons, specifically Kshell

1151
00:43:55,680 --> 00:44:00,000
electrons, the attenuation suddenly

1152
00:43:57,599 --> 00:44:02,000
increases. This produces a sharp jump in

1153
00:44:00,000 --> 00:44:05,680
the attenuation curve known as the K

1154
00:44:02,000 --> 00:44:07,839
edge. This feature is element specific.

1155
00:44:05,680 --> 00:44:10,079
Each element has its own characteristic

1156
00:44:07,839 --> 00:44:13,040
K edge energy. For example, in gold, the

1157
00:44:10,079 --> 00:44:15,119
K edge occurs at about 80.7 KV. That

1158
00:44:13,040 --> 00:44:17,520
means if the X-ray photon energy just

1159
00:44:15,119 --> 00:44:19,119
crosses this threshold, gold absorbs

1160
00:44:17,520 --> 00:44:21,680
much more strongly due to the

1161
00:44:19,119 --> 00:44:23,440
photoelectric effect.

1162
00:44:21,680 --> 00:44:25,680
This is very powerful because it allows

1163
00:44:23,440 --> 00:44:27,839
us to do chemically specific imaging. If

1164
00:44:25,680 --> 00:44:30,240
we use detectors that can resolve X-ray

1165
00:44:27,839 --> 00:44:32,240
energy, we can identify where particular

1166
00:44:30,240 --> 00:44:34,800
elements are present based on their K

1167
00:44:32,240 --> 00:44:36,560
edge signatures. This opens the door to

1168
00:44:34,800 --> 00:44:39,359
material decomposition and even

1169
00:44:36,560 --> 00:44:42,000
molecular level imaging. In current

1170
00:44:39,359 --> 00:44:44,480
hospital CT systems, detectors typically

1171
00:44:42,000 --> 00:44:46,400
integrate over all energies. That's like

1172
00:44:44,480 --> 00:44:48,800
measuring the total area under the

1173
00:44:46,400 --> 00:44:50,880
attenuation curve. You get a grayscale

1174
00:44:48,800 --> 00:44:52,720
value, but not the details of the energy

1175
00:44:50,880 --> 00:44:54,960
dependence.

1176
00:44:52,720 --> 00:44:56,880
However, with modern energy sensitive

1177
00:44:54,960 --> 00:44:58,880
detectors, we can directly measure the

1178
00:44:56,880 --> 00:45:00,160
shape of the curve. That means we can

1179
00:44:58,880 --> 00:45:02,319
distinguish materials that would

1180
00:45:00,160 --> 00:45:04,480
otherwise look similar in a conventional

1181
00:45:02,319 --> 00:45:06,560
CT scan.

1182
00:45:04,480 --> 00:45:09,520
This concept is especially important for

1183
00:45:06,560 --> 00:45:12,000
contrast agents. Elements like iodine,

1184
00:45:09,520 --> 00:45:15,200
gatalinium, and gold nanop particles all

1185
00:45:12,000 --> 00:45:17,280
have distinct K edges. With energy

1186
00:45:15,200 --> 00:45:19,760
resolved imaging, we can separate their

1187
00:45:17,280 --> 00:45:21,280
signals, perform quantitative mapping,

1188
00:45:19,760 --> 00:45:23,520
and even design targeted

1189
00:45:21,280 --> 00:45:26,240
nanoparticlebased contrast agents for

1190
00:45:23,520 --> 00:45:28,079
advanced molecular imaging.

1191
00:45:26,240 --> 00:45:30,160
So the K edge is not just a physics

1192
00:45:28,079 --> 00:45:32,480
detail, it's the foundation for the

1193
00:45:30,160 --> 00:45:35,960
future of spectral CT and molecular

1194
00:45:32,480 --> 00:45:35,960
X-ray imaging.

1195
00:45:37,280 --> 00:45:41,440
Now let's look at how contrast agents

1196
00:45:39,280 --> 00:45:43,359
take advantage of the K edge effect we

1197
00:45:41,440 --> 00:45:45,839
just discussed.

1198
00:45:43,359 --> 00:45:48,400
On this plot we see the mass attenuation

1199
00:45:45,839 --> 00:45:51,440
curves for different substances. Lipid,

1200
00:45:48,400 --> 00:45:54,400
water, bone, iodine, gatalinium, and

1201
00:45:51,440 --> 00:45:56,160
gold. Notice that lipid, water, and bone

1202
00:45:54,400 --> 00:45:58,480
have smooth curves that gradually

1203
00:45:56,160 --> 00:46:00,400
decrease as energy increases. Without

1204
00:45:58,480 --> 00:46:02,640
extra information, their signals can

1205
00:46:00,400 --> 00:46:06,079
overlap, and it's very difficult to tell

1206
00:46:02,640 --> 00:46:08,640
them apart. But look closely at iodine,

1207
00:46:06,079 --> 00:46:10,400
gatalinium, and gold. Each has a sharp

1208
00:46:08,640 --> 00:46:13,040
jump in attenuation at its

1209
00:46:10,400 --> 00:46:15,680
characteristic kage energy. Iodine has

1210
00:46:13,040 --> 00:46:18,400
its edge at about 33 kev, gatalinium

1211
00:46:15,680 --> 00:46:20,640
around 50 kev, and gold at about 80 kev.

1212
00:46:18,400 --> 00:46:23,200
These discontinuities are like chemical

1213
00:46:20,640 --> 00:46:25,440
fingerprints.

1214
00:46:23,200 --> 00:46:27,280
If your detector simply integrates all

1215
00:46:25,440 --> 00:46:28,960
energies, you cannot distinguish whether

1216
00:46:27,280 --> 00:46:31,760
the bright signal you see comes from

1217
00:46:28,960 --> 00:46:33,920
iodine or from gold or even a mixture of

1218
00:46:31,760 --> 00:46:35,680
bone and water.

1219
00:46:33,920 --> 00:46:38,079
But if you can measure the attenuation

1220
00:46:35,680 --> 00:46:40,079
as a function of energy, in other words,

1221
00:46:38,079 --> 00:46:42,079
if you can see the shape of this curve,

1222
00:46:40,079 --> 00:46:44,079
then you can identify which element is

1223
00:46:42,079 --> 00:46:46,720
responsible.

1224
00:46:44,079 --> 00:46:49,040
This is the basis of KGE imaging. By

1225
00:46:46,720 --> 00:46:50,640
designing energy sensitive detectors, we

1226
00:46:49,040 --> 00:46:52,800
can separate the signals of different

1227
00:46:50,640 --> 00:46:55,119
contrast agents.

1228
00:46:52,800 --> 00:46:58,160
Clinically, iodine is already the most

1229
00:46:55,119 --> 00:47:00,880
common CT contrast agent. Gatalinium

1230
00:46:58,160 --> 00:47:03,920
which is widely used in MRI can also be

1231
00:47:00,880 --> 00:47:05,440
detected in spectral CT and gold nano

1232
00:47:03,920 --> 00:47:07,040
particles are being investigated for

1233
00:47:05,440 --> 00:47:09,119
advanced applications including

1234
00:47:07,040 --> 00:47:11,200
molecular imaging and drug delivery

1235
00:47:09,119 --> 00:47:13,359
because of their strong K edge at higher

1236
00:47:11,200 --> 00:47:15,760
energies.

1237
00:47:13,359 --> 00:47:18,720
So the key message here is traditional

1238
00:47:15,760 --> 00:47:21,200
CT shows only grayscale intensity, but

1239
00:47:18,720 --> 00:47:23,599
spectral CT can unlock these curves and

1240
00:47:21,200 --> 00:47:25,680
make imaging chemically specific. That

1241
00:47:23,599 --> 00:47:27,760
allows us not only to see anatomy, but

1242
00:47:25,680 --> 00:47:32,520
also to distinguish materials and track

1243
00:47:27,760 --> 00:47:32,520
contrast agents at the molecular level.

1244
00:47:33,839 --> 00:47:38,480
Now we come to the final part of today's

1245
00:47:35,680 --> 00:47:40,319
lecture, the X-ray detector.

1246
00:47:38,480 --> 00:47:42,160
In your textbook, you will see quite a

1247
00:47:40,319 --> 00:47:44,079
bit of discussion about traditional film

1248
00:47:42,160 --> 00:47:45,839
and early detection methods. Those are

1249
00:47:44,079 --> 00:47:47,119
historically important and I encourage

1250
00:47:45,839 --> 00:47:48,720
you to read them so you have general

1251
00:47:47,119 --> 00:47:51,280
knowledge. But in practice, those

1252
00:47:48,720 --> 00:47:52,960
methods are now outdated. What really

1253
00:47:51,280 --> 00:47:55,200
matters for us is how X-rays are

1254
00:47:52,960 --> 00:47:56,720
detected in modern CT scanners and what

1255
00:47:55,200 --> 00:47:59,599
technologies are shaping the future of

1256
00:47:56,720 --> 00:48:01,760
X-ray imaging. Modern detectors are

1257
00:47:59,599 --> 00:48:03,839
digital. They convert incoming X-ray

1258
00:48:01,760 --> 00:48:06,160
photons into electrical signals that can

1259
00:48:03,839 --> 00:48:08,000
be processed by the computer. And today

1260
00:48:06,160 --> 00:48:10,960
there are two main classes of detector

1261
00:48:08,000 --> 00:48:13,280
technologies that you should understand.

1262
00:48:10,960 --> 00:48:15,280
Energy integrating detectors. This is

1263
00:48:13,280 --> 00:48:17,920
the technology used in nearly all

1264
00:48:15,280 --> 00:48:20,079
current CT scanners. They simply collect

1265
00:48:17,920 --> 00:48:23,200
all the incoming photons and integrate

1266
00:48:20,079 --> 00:48:25,520
their total energy. Whether a photon has

1267
00:48:23,200 --> 00:48:27,280
high energy or low energy, the detector

1268
00:48:25,520 --> 00:48:29,040
just sums them up. That gives us

1269
00:48:27,280 --> 00:48:31,760
grayscale images, the kind you are

1270
00:48:29,040 --> 00:48:33,760
familiar with in clinical CT.

1271
00:48:31,760 --> 00:48:35,520
A variation of this is dual energy

1272
00:48:33,760 --> 00:48:37,440
imaging where two different energy

1273
00:48:35,520 --> 00:48:40,559
spectra are used to give some limited

1274
00:48:37,440 --> 00:48:42,480
energy discrimination. Dual energy CT is

1275
00:48:40,559 --> 00:48:44,160
already widely applied. For example, to

1276
00:48:42,480 --> 00:48:45,920
distinguish iodine contrast from

1277
00:48:44,160 --> 00:48:48,480
calcium.

1278
00:48:45,920 --> 00:48:50,960
Photon counting detectors. This is the

1279
00:48:48,480 --> 00:48:52,559
emerging next generation technology.

1280
00:48:50,960 --> 00:48:54,640
Instead of summing all energies

1281
00:48:52,559 --> 00:48:56,240
together, photon counting detectors

1282
00:48:54,640 --> 00:48:59,440
measure each incoming photon

1283
00:48:56,240 --> 00:49:01,200
individually and record its energy.

1284
00:48:59,440 --> 00:49:03,119
That means they can separate photons

1285
00:49:01,200 --> 00:49:04,800
into different energy bins giving us

1286
00:49:03,119 --> 00:49:06,240
spectral information. With this

1287
00:49:04,800 --> 00:49:08,559
approach, we can do material

1288
00:49:06,240 --> 00:49:10,800
decomposition, kage imaging and

1289
00:49:08,559 --> 00:49:13,839
molecular level x-ray imaging which we

1290
00:49:10,800 --> 00:49:16,559
just discussed with gold and gatalinium.

1291
00:49:13,839 --> 00:49:18,640
So to summarize, energy integrating

1292
00:49:16,559 --> 00:49:20,559
detectors are the current standard while

1293
00:49:18,640 --> 00:49:22,960
photon counting detectors represent the

1294
00:49:20,559 --> 00:49:25,119
future. They promise higher resolution,

1295
00:49:22,960 --> 00:49:26,880
lower dose and much richer information

1296
00:49:25,119 --> 00:49:28,880
content.

1297
00:49:26,880 --> 00:49:30,960
This transition from energy integration

1298
00:49:28,880 --> 00:49:32,960
to photon counting is one of the most

1299
00:49:30,960 --> 00:49:36,920
exciting directions in X-ray imaging

1300
00:49:32,960 --> 00:49:36,920
research and clinical translation.

1301
00:49:38,400 --> 00:49:42,240
Energy integrating detectors are the

1302
00:49:40,240 --> 00:49:45,839
most common, practical, and mature

1303
00:49:42,240 --> 00:49:48,000
technology used in modern CT scanners.

1304
00:49:45,839 --> 00:49:49,920
Their principle is very simple. They

1305
00:49:48,000 --> 00:49:51,920
collect the total energy deposited by

1306
00:49:49,920 --> 00:49:53,680
all X-ray photons that arrive at the

1307
00:49:51,920 --> 00:49:55,680
detector element. It doesn't matter

1308
00:49:53,680 --> 00:49:58,079
whether the incoming photons are high

1309
00:49:55,680 --> 00:50:00,720
energy or low energy. In the end, they

1310
00:49:58,079 --> 00:50:04,079
are all added together.

1311
00:50:00,720 --> 00:50:07,359
Now, this technology works in two modes.

1312
00:50:04,079 --> 00:50:09,599
Indirect mode. In indirect mode, the

1313
00:50:07,359 --> 00:50:11,760
X-ray photons first pass through a layer

1314
00:50:09,599 --> 00:50:14,319
of material called a phosphor such as

1315
00:50:11,760 --> 00:50:16,240
cesium iodide. The phosphor absorbs the

1316
00:50:14,319 --> 00:50:18,240
x-ray energy and converts it into

1317
00:50:16,240 --> 00:50:21,359
visible light. Each x-ray photon

1318
00:50:18,240 --> 00:50:23,440
produces many visible light photons.

1319
00:50:21,359 --> 00:50:25,280
These visible photons then strike a

1320
00:50:23,440 --> 00:50:27,119
photo diode layer which converts the

1321
00:50:25,280 --> 00:50:29,119
light into an electrical current. The

1322
00:50:27,119 --> 00:50:31,359
current is then read out by the circuit.

1323
00:50:29,119 --> 00:50:33,599
In this process, higher energy X-ray

1324
00:50:31,359 --> 00:50:35,680
photons produce more visible light. But

1325
00:50:33,599 --> 00:50:37,520
once converted to charge, all signals

1326
00:50:35,680 --> 00:50:39,599
are summed together. That's why this

1327
00:50:37,520 --> 00:50:42,480
method is called energy integrating or

1328
00:50:39,599 --> 00:50:45,920
sometimes current integrating.

1329
00:50:42,480 --> 00:50:48,240
Direct mode. In direct mode, there is no

1330
00:50:45,920 --> 00:50:50,079
intermediate light conversion. Instead,

1331
00:50:48,240 --> 00:50:52,400
the X-rays interact directly with a

1332
00:50:50,079 --> 00:50:54,720
semiconductor material such as amorphous

1333
00:50:52,400 --> 00:50:56,720
selenium. The X-ray photons generate

1334
00:50:54,720 --> 00:50:58,800
electron whole pairs in the material.

1335
00:50:56,720 --> 00:51:00,559
Under a high voltage bias, these charges

1336
00:50:58,800 --> 00:51:02,480
are separated and collected as an

1337
00:51:00,559 --> 00:51:04,160
electrical current.

1338
00:51:02,480 --> 00:51:06,160
Again, the detector integrates all

1339
00:51:04,160 --> 00:51:08,160
charges into one signal, which

1340
00:51:06,160 --> 00:51:10,720
represents the total X-ray energy

1341
00:51:08,160 --> 00:51:12,800
deposited.

1342
00:51:10,720 --> 00:51:14,319
So, in both indirect and direct modes,

1343
00:51:12,800 --> 00:51:17,040
the detector does not distinguish

1344
00:51:14,319 --> 00:51:18,800
between high and low energy photons. It

1345
00:51:17,040 --> 00:51:21,040
simply reports a single number

1346
00:51:18,800 --> 00:51:23,359
proportional to the total energy of all

1347
00:51:21,040 --> 00:51:25,359
photons that pass through the patient.

1348
00:51:23,359 --> 00:51:28,400
This gives us the grayscale images we

1349
00:51:25,359 --> 00:51:30,480
are familiar with in clinical CT. This

1350
00:51:28,400 --> 00:51:32,240
approach is robust, efficient, and

1351
00:51:30,480 --> 00:51:36,680
reliable. And it has been the backbone

1352
00:51:32,240 --> 00:51:36,680
of medical CT imaging for decades.

1353
00:51:38,240 --> 00:51:41,680
Now, let's talk about an important

1354
00:51:39,839 --> 00:51:44,720
concept in X-ray imaging, the

1355
00:51:41,680 --> 00:51:46,800
anti-catter grid. As we discussed

1356
00:51:44,720 --> 00:51:49,119
earlier, when X-rays interact with the

1357
00:51:46,800 --> 00:51:51,680
body, two main processes occur.

1358
00:51:49,119 --> 00:51:54,000
Photoelectric absorption and scattering.

1359
00:51:51,680 --> 00:51:56,079
In medical X-rays, scattering is very

1360
00:51:54,000 --> 00:51:58,079
common. That means many photons change

1361
00:51:56,079 --> 00:51:59,599
direction inside the body and then try

1362
00:51:58,079 --> 00:52:01,119
to reach the detector from different

1363
00:51:59,599 --> 00:52:03,119
angles.

1364
00:52:01,119 --> 00:52:05,520
But here's the problem. What we really

1365
00:52:03,119 --> 00:52:07,599
want to measure is the primary beam. The

1366
00:52:05,520 --> 00:52:09,520
photons that travel straight along the

1367
00:52:07,599 --> 00:52:11,280
original X-ray path. These are the

1368
00:52:09,520 --> 00:52:13,200
photons that contain the true line

1369
00:52:11,280 --> 00:52:15,760
integral information we need for

1370
00:52:13,200 --> 00:52:18,000
accurate imaging. If scattered photons

1371
00:52:15,760 --> 00:52:20,400
are also recorded, they blur the image

1372
00:52:18,000 --> 00:52:22,559
and reduce contrast.

1373
00:52:20,400 --> 00:52:24,480
To solve this, we use an anti-catter

1374
00:52:22,559 --> 00:52:26,960
grid. You can think of it as a sheet

1375
00:52:24,480 --> 00:52:29,200
with many tiny channels, almost like

1376
00:52:26,960 --> 00:52:30,800
thousands of pin holes. Only photons

1377
00:52:29,200 --> 00:52:32,880
traveling in the correct direction

1378
00:52:30,800 --> 00:52:34,319
aligned with the primary X-ray beam can

1379
00:52:32,880 --> 00:52:36,240
pass through these channels and reach

1380
00:52:34,319 --> 00:52:38,640
the detector.

1381
00:52:36,240 --> 00:52:40,559
Scattered photons coming in at an angle

1382
00:52:38,640 --> 00:52:43,359
are blocked by the grid walls which are

1383
00:52:40,559 --> 00:52:45,520
made of dense materials such as lead.

1384
00:52:43,359 --> 00:52:47,680
This dramatically improves image quality

1385
00:52:45,520 --> 00:52:49,520
by reducing the effect of scatter. Of

1386
00:52:47,680 --> 00:52:51,680
course, there are trade-offs. If the

1387
00:52:49,520 --> 00:52:53,440
grid is very tall or tightly spaced, it

1388
00:52:51,680 --> 00:52:55,119
rejects more scatter, but it can also

1389
00:52:53,440 --> 00:52:57,280
block some of the primary beam, which

1390
00:52:55,119 --> 00:52:59,200
reduces efficiency.

1391
00:52:57,280 --> 00:53:01,839
Engineers must carefully design the

1392
00:52:59,200 --> 00:53:03,359
grid's height, spacing, and material to

1393
00:53:01,839 --> 00:53:05,200
strike a balance between scatter

1394
00:53:03,359 --> 00:53:06,960
rejection and preserving the useful

1395
00:53:05,200 --> 00:53:09,599
signal.

1396
00:53:06,960 --> 00:53:11,599
So, in summary, the anti-catter grid is

1397
00:53:09,599 --> 00:53:13,440
a simple but powerful device that

1398
00:53:11,599 --> 00:53:15,760
ensures we measure mostly the primary

1399
00:53:13,440 --> 00:53:19,240
beam, giving us much clearer and more

1400
00:53:15,760 --> 00:53:19,240
reliable images.

1401
00:53:20,720 --> 00:53:24,559
Here you can clearly see the difference

1402
00:53:22,319 --> 00:53:27,440
between using an anti-catter grid and

1403
00:53:24,559 --> 00:53:30,000
not using one. On the left with the

1404
00:53:27,440 --> 00:53:32,319
grid, the anatomical structures are much

1405
00:53:30,000 --> 00:53:34,400
sharper. The boundaries of the ribs, the

1406
00:53:32,319 --> 00:53:36,880
lung fields, and even the soft tissues

1407
00:53:34,400 --> 00:53:38,720
are more clearly defined.

1408
00:53:36,880 --> 00:53:40,960
On the right, without the grid, the

1409
00:53:38,720 --> 00:53:42,559
image looks more foggy. That haziness

1410
00:53:40,960 --> 00:53:44,079
comes from scattered photons that are

1411
00:53:42,559 --> 00:53:46,160
superimposed on top of the fine

1412
00:53:44,079 --> 00:53:48,400
structures. Essentially, the scatter

1413
00:53:46,160 --> 00:53:50,079
washes out important details and reduces

1414
00:53:48,400 --> 00:53:51,440
contrast.

1415
00:53:50,079 --> 00:53:53,920
Think of it like looking out of an

1416
00:53:51,440 --> 00:53:56,559
airplane. On a clear day, you can see

1417
00:53:53,920 --> 00:53:59,040
the ground with sharp detail. But on a

1418
00:53:56,559 --> 00:54:01,760
cloudy day, the clouds blur your view.

1419
00:53:59,040 --> 00:54:04,000
In the same way, scattered X-ray photons

1420
00:54:01,760 --> 00:54:06,880
act like a layer of clouds, blurring the

1421
00:54:04,000 --> 00:54:08,640
image and hiding subtle differences.

1422
00:54:06,880 --> 00:54:11,040
This becomes especially important when

1423
00:54:08,640 --> 00:54:12,800
detecting low contrast tumors. Without a

1424
00:54:11,040 --> 00:54:14,800
grid, these tumors might be completely

1425
00:54:12,800 --> 00:54:16,800
hidden in the fog of scatter. With a

1426
00:54:14,800 --> 00:54:18,640
grid, however, you remove much of that

1427
00:54:16,800 --> 00:54:21,200
scatter, revealing the underlying

1428
00:54:18,640 --> 00:54:23,520
details more accurately.

1429
00:54:21,200 --> 00:54:25,040
So using an anti-catter grid can make

1430
00:54:23,520 --> 00:54:29,640
the difference between detecting a

1431
00:54:25,040 --> 00:54:29,640
disease early and missing it entirely.

1432
00:54:31,440 --> 00:54:35,119
Now let's dive into dual energy imaging

1433
00:54:33,760 --> 00:54:37,839
which is one of the most important

1434
00:54:35,119 --> 00:54:41,040
developments in modern CT.

1435
00:54:37,839 --> 00:54:44,240
All the major CT manufacturers Seammens,

1436
00:54:41,040 --> 00:54:46,160
J, Philips, and Toshiba have worked on

1437
00:54:44,240 --> 00:54:48,000
energy sensitive imaging. While true

1438
00:54:46,160 --> 00:54:49,839
multi-energy imaging is still a

1439
00:54:48,000 --> 00:54:51,680
challenge, they have all developed

1440
00:54:49,839 --> 00:54:54,079
practical approaches to perform dual

1441
00:54:51,680 --> 00:54:56,960
energy CT, which means collecting data

1442
00:54:54,079 --> 00:54:59,280
using two different X-ray spectra.

1443
00:54:56,960 --> 00:55:02,160
Let me explain the main strategies.

1444
00:54:59,280 --> 00:55:04,400
Seammens uses dual source CT. Here the

1445
00:55:02,160 --> 00:55:06,640
scanner has two X-ray tubes and two

1446
00:55:04,400 --> 00:55:08,559
detectors mounted at an angle, each

1447
00:55:06,640 --> 00:55:11,680
operating at a different voltage. For

1448
00:55:08,559 --> 00:55:15,520
example, one tube might run at 80 kts

1449
00:55:11,680 --> 00:55:16,880
while the other runs at 140 kts.

1450
00:55:15,520 --> 00:55:18,960
This gives you two spectra

1451
00:55:16,880 --> 00:55:21,119
simultaneously which means excellent

1452
00:55:18,960 --> 00:55:22,720
temporal resolution and minimal motion

1453
00:55:21,119 --> 00:55:25,520
artifact.

1454
00:55:22,720 --> 00:55:27,760
Ghe uses KVP switching. In this method,

1455
00:55:25,520 --> 00:55:29,680
there is only one X-ray tube, but the

1456
00:55:27,760 --> 00:55:33,119
tube rapidly alternates its voltage

1457
00:55:29,680 --> 00:55:36,880
between high and low values, say 140 and

1458
00:55:33,119 --> 00:55:38,960
80 ks from one projection to the next.

1459
00:55:36,880 --> 00:55:41,040
As a result, you still get two distinct

1460
00:55:38,960 --> 00:55:43,119
spectra, but collected sequentially.

1461
00:55:41,040 --> 00:55:45,040
Philips uses dual layer detectors.

1462
00:55:43,119 --> 00:55:47,359
Instead of modifying the tube, they

1463
00:55:45,040 --> 00:55:49,520
modify the detector.

1464
00:55:47,359 --> 00:55:51,680
The top layer absorbs most of the low

1465
00:55:49,520 --> 00:55:53,599
energy X-rays while the second layer

1466
00:55:51,680 --> 00:55:55,839
captures the higher energy photons that

1467
00:55:53,599 --> 00:55:57,839
pass through. This way, low and high

1468
00:55:55,839 --> 00:56:00,319
energy data are naturally separated at

1469
00:55:57,839 --> 00:56:02,799
the detector level.

1470
00:56:00,319 --> 00:56:04,799
Toshiba uses dual scan mode. In this

1471
00:56:02,799 --> 00:56:06,799
case, the scanner acquires two full

1472
00:56:04,799 --> 00:56:09,760
scans of the same patient, one at high

1473
00:56:06,799 --> 00:56:12,000
voltage and one at low voltage. While

1474
00:56:09,760 --> 00:56:14,319
this is simpler in concept, it has

1475
00:56:12,000 --> 00:56:17,119
drawbacks. It takes longer and the

1476
00:56:14,319 --> 00:56:19,200
patient may move between scans. Also, if

1477
00:56:17,119 --> 00:56:20,880
contrast material is injected, its

1478
00:56:19,200 --> 00:56:24,000
distribution may change between the two

1479
00:56:20,880 --> 00:56:25,599
acquisitions leading to misregistration.

1480
00:56:24,000 --> 00:56:27,920
So, in practice, the first three

1481
00:56:25,599 --> 00:56:29,920
technologies, dual source, KVP

1482
00:56:27,920 --> 00:56:32,079
switching, and dual layer detectors are

1483
00:56:29,920 --> 00:56:33,839
more effective. They provide two sets of

1484
00:56:32,079 --> 00:56:36,160
spectral information within the same

1485
00:56:33,839 --> 00:56:38,559
exam, which can then be used to separate

1486
00:56:36,160 --> 00:56:40,240
materials, improve contrast, and reduce

1487
00:56:38,559 --> 00:56:42,319
artifacts.

1488
00:56:40,240 --> 00:56:44,960
This is why dual energy imaging is now

1489
00:56:42,319 --> 00:56:46,799
widely used in clinical practice. It

1490
00:56:44,960 --> 00:56:49,119
gives radiologists much more information

1491
00:56:46,799 --> 00:56:50,720
than standard CT and opens the door to

1492
00:56:49,119 --> 00:56:52,720
advanced applications like material

1493
00:56:50,720 --> 00:56:55,119
decomposition and virtual contrast

1494
00:56:52,720 --> 00:56:57,520
imaging.

1495
00:56:55,119 --> 00:56:59,520
Before

1496
00:56:57,520 --> 00:57:01,599
we go further into dual energy imaging,

1497
00:56:59,520 --> 00:57:03,920
let's pause for a quick review of some

1498
00:57:01,599 --> 00:57:06,240
basic chemistry concepts, specifically

1499
00:57:03,920 --> 00:57:07,920
the atomic number and the mass number.

1500
00:57:06,240 --> 00:57:11,280
Because these concepts form the physical

1501
00:57:07,920 --> 00:57:13,760
foundation of dual energy CT, the atomic

1502
00:57:11,280 --> 00:57:15,520
number, usually denoted by Z, is simply

1503
00:57:13,760 --> 00:57:18,160
the number of protons in the nucleus of

1504
00:57:15,520 --> 00:57:21,520
an atom. It defines the identity of the

1505
00:57:18,160 --> 00:57:25,280
element. For example, hydrogen has Z= 1.

1506
00:57:21,520 --> 00:57:28,559
Helium has Z= 2. Carbon has Z= 6 and so

1507
00:57:25,280 --> 00:57:30,559
on. If you change Z, you change the

1508
00:57:28,559 --> 00:57:32,960
element itself.

1509
00:57:30,559 --> 00:57:34,640
The mass number often written as a is

1510
00:57:32,960 --> 00:57:37,280
approximately the total number of

1511
00:57:34,640 --> 00:57:39,280
protons plus neutrons in the nucleus.

1512
00:57:37,280 --> 00:57:42,000
Since neutrons contribute to the mass

1513
00:57:39,280 --> 00:57:45,040
but not the charge, A is usually larger

1514
00:57:42,000 --> 00:57:47,920
than Z. Take helium as an example.

1515
00:57:45,040 --> 00:57:50,400
Helium has Z equals 2, meaning two

1516
00:57:47,920 --> 00:57:52,640
protons. Most stable helium atoms also

1517
00:57:50,400 --> 00:57:55,599
have two neutrons, giving a mass number

1518
00:57:52,640 --> 00:57:57,040
of A= 4. That's why you see the helium

1519
00:57:55,599 --> 00:57:59,920
symbol here written with a two at the

1520
00:57:57,040 --> 00:58:03,119
bottom and a four at the top. So why is

1521
00:57:59,920 --> 00:58:04,799
this important for us?

1522
00:58:03,119 --> 00:58:06,799
Because the way X-rays interact with

1523
00:58:04,799 --> 00:58:08,799
matter, whether through photoelectric

1524
00:58:06,799 --> 00:58:10,559
absorption or Compton scattering,

1525
00:58:08,799 --> 00:58:13,280
depends strongly on both the atomic

1526
00:58:10,559 --> 00:58:15,920
number and the mass of the element. High

1527
00:58:13,280 --> 00:58:18,960
atomic number elements like iodine,

1528
00:58:15,920 --> 00:58:20,960
gatalinium or gold interact with x-rays

1529
00:58:18,960 --> 00:58:24,480
very differently from soft tissue which

1530
00:58:20,960 --> 00:58:26,400
is mostly carbon, oxygen and hydrogen.

1531
00:58:24,480 --> 00:58:28,640
And this difference is exactly what dual

1532
00:58:26,400 --> 00:58:30,799
energy imaging exploits. By probing

1533
00:58:28,640 --> 00:58:32,640
tissues at two different energy levels,

1534
00:58:30,799 --> 00:58:34,480
we can begin to separate and even

1535
00:58:32,640 --> 00:58:37,480
identify different materials inside the

1536
00:58:34,480 --> 00:58:37,480
body.

1537
00:58:38,960 --> 00:58:44,079
So here is a classic expression for the

1538
00:58:41,119 --> 00:58:46,079
X-ray attenuation coefficient.

1539
00:58:44,079 --> 00:58:48,240
The main idea is that attenuation comes

1540
00:58:46,079 --> 00:58:50,079
from two parts. One part is the

1541
00:58:48,240 --> 00:58:52,720
photoelectric effect and the other part

1542
00:58:50,079 --> 00:58:54,640
is compant scattering.

1543
00:58:52,720 --> 00:58:57,520
The photoelectric effect is very

1544
00:58:54,640 --> 00:58:59,520
strongly dependent on energy. In fact as

1545
00:58:57,520 --> 00:59:01,280
the X-ray energy increases the

1546
00:58:59,520 --> 00:59:03,440
contribution from the photoelectric

1547
00:59:01,280 --> 00:59:05,680
effect drops very quickly. That's why

1548
00:59:03,440 --> 00:59:07,440
low energy X-rays are absorbed much

1549
00:59:05,680 --> 00:59:09,440
more.

1550
00:59:07,440 --> 00:59:11,520
The Compton scattering term on the other

1551
00:59:09,440 --> 00:59:13,760
hand has a more gradual dependence on

1552
00:59:11,520 --> 00:59:16,240
energy. It comes from what is called the

1553
00:59:13,760 --> 00:59:18,720
Klein Nisha function which describes how

1554
00:59:16,240 --> 00:59:20,559
photons scatter off electrons. So if you

1555
00:59:18,720 --> 00:59:22,880
put these two terms together, you can

1556
00:59:20,559 --> 00:59:25,119
describe the full attenuation as a

1557
00:59:22,880 --> 00:59:28,000
combination of photoelectric absorption

1558
00:59:25,119 --> 00:59:30,240
and Compton scattering. Now here's the

1559
00:59:28,000 --> 00:59:32,400
powerful part. If we measure attenuation

1560
00:59:30,240 --> 00:59:34,799
at two different X-ray energy spectra,

1561
00:59:32,400 --> 00:59:36,880
we can separate these two contributions.

1562
00:59:34,799 --> 00:59:38,880
Once that's done, we can predict how the

1563
00:59:36,880 --> 00:59:40,559
material would behave at any other

1564
00:59:38,880 --> 00:59:42,880
energy.

1565
00:59:40,559 --> 00:59:44,319
You can think about this in two ways.

1566
00:59:42,880 --> 00:59:46,160
Physically, we are separating

1567
00:59:44,319 --> 00:59:50,079
attenuation into its two main

1568
00:59:46,160 --> 00:59:51,920
mechanisms, photoelectric and compton.

1569
00:59:50,079 --> 00:59:53,520
Material-wise, we are saying that the

1570
00:59:51,920 --> 00:59:56,319
body can be thought of as a mixture of

1571
00:59:53,520 --> 00:59:58,880
two basic materials. For example, water

1572
00:59:56,319 --> 01:00:00,960
and bone. Any other tissue can be

1573
00:59:58,880 --> 01:00:02,400
represented as a combination of those

1574
01:00:00,960 --> 01:00:04,960
two.

1575
01:00:02,400 --> 01:00:07,200
That's the foundation of dual energy CT.

1576
01:00:04,960 --> 01:00:09,280
By using two X-ray spectra, we can get

1577
01:00:07,200 --> 01:00:12,839
material specific information, not just

1578
01:00:09,280 --> 01:00:12,839
grayscale attenuation.

1579
01:00:14,559 --> 01:00:19,440
So, how do we actually perform a dual

1580
01:00:16,559 --> 01:00:21,200
energy measurement?

1581
01:00:19,440 --> 01:00:23,440
The key is that we collect data using

1582
01:00:21,200 --> 01:00:26,400
two different X-ray spectra. Let's call

1583
01:00:23,440 --> 01:00:28,319
them spectrum one and spectrum 2. Now,

1584
01:00:26,400 --> 01:00:30,079
recall that the attenuation coefficient

1585
01:00:28,319 --> 01:00:31,920
has two main components. the

1586
01:00:30,079 --> 01:00:33,680
photoelectric part and the Compton

1587
01:00:31,920 --> 01:00:36,000
scattering part. When we take the line

1588
01:00:33,680 --> 01:00:37,839
integral along an X-ray path, we can

1589
01:00:36,000 --> 01:00:40,079
separate these contributions into two

1590
01:00:37,839 --> 01:00:41,599
quantities. We usually call them A1 and

1591
01:00:40,079 --> 01:00:44,000
A2.

1592
01:00:41,599 --> 01:00:45,599
So what happens is this. If we measure

1593
01:00:44,000 --> 01:00:48,160
the transmitted X-ray signal with

1594
01:00:45,599 --> 01:00:50,480
spectrum 1, we get one equation that

1595
01:00:48,160 --> 01:00:52,559
depends on A1 and A2. If we measure

1596
01:00:50,480 --> 01:00:54,079
again with spectrum 2, we get a second

1597
01:00:52,559 --> 01:00:56,000
equation.

1598
01:00:54,079 --> 01:00:58,319
Now we have two equations and two

1599
01:00:56,000 --> 01:01:01,599
unknowns. That means we can solve for A1

1600
01:00:58,319 --> 01:01:03,119
and A2. And once we know A1 and A2, we

1601
01:01:01,599 --> 01:01:06,240
can reconstruct the attenuation

1602
01:01:03,119 --> 01:01:08,559
coefficient at any X-ray energy.

1603
01:01:06,240 --> 01:01:10,400
That's the whole idea of dual energy CT.

1604
01:01:08,559 --> 01:01:12,079
By using two spectra, we get enough

1605
01:01:10,400 --> 01:01:14,559
independent information to separate the

1606
01:01:12,079 --> 01:01:16,000
photoelectric and Compton contributions.

1607
01:01:14,559 --> 01:01:18,079
And from there, we can start to do

1608
01:01:16,000 --> 01:01:20,079
material decomposition, distinguishing

1609
01:01:18,079 --> 01:01:23,200
water from bone, or even identifying

1610
01:01:20,079 --> 01:01:25,200
contrast agents like iodine or gold. So

1611
01:01:23,200 --> 01:01:27,359
dual energy imaging is not just a trick

1612
01:01:25,200 --> 01:01:31,640
with hardware. It has a very solid

1613
01:01:27,359 --> 01:01:31,640
physical and mathematical foundation.

1614
01:01:33,280 --> 01:01:38,960
Now, let's move to the last topic in

1615
01:01:35,280 --> 01:01:40,799
this section, photon counting CT.

1616
01:01:38,960 --> 01:01:43,040
Up to this point, we've mostly been

1617
01:01:40,799 --> 01:01:44,319
talking about conventional detectors.

1618
01:01:43,040 --> 01:01:46,400
Those are what we call current

1619
01:01:44,319 --> 01:01:48,559
integrating detectors. What they really

1620
01:01:46,400 --> 01:01:50,480
do is add up all the incoming X-ray

1621
01:01:48,559 --> 01:01:54,160
photons across the entire energy

1622
01:01:50,480 --> 01:01:56,160
spectrum and then report one number. In

1623
01:01:54,160 --> 01:01:58,240
other words, they just give us the area

1624
01:01:56,160 --> 01:02:00,720
under the curve. That means we only get

1625
01:01:58,240 --> 01:02:03,040
a grayscale image, essentially black and

1626
01:02:00,720 --> 01:02:04,799
white, where each pixel represents total

1627
01:02:03,040 --> 01:02:07,040
intensity.

1628
01:02:04,799 --> 01:02:09,359
But imagine if instead of throwing all

1629
01:02:07,040 --> 01:02:11,040
the energy information into one basket,

1630
01:02:09,359 --> 01:02:13,760
we could measure the energy of each

1631
01:02:11,040 --> 01:02:15,520
photon. That's the principle of photon

1632
01:02:13,760 --> 01:02:17,760
counting detectors. Instead of giving

1633
01:02:15,520 --> 01:02:20,400
you one number, they break the spectrum

1634
01:02:17,760 --> 01:02:22,640
into many energy bins.

1635
01:02:20,400 --> 01:02:24,960
So now instead of saying this voxil

1636
01:02:22,640 --> 01:02:27,119
attenuates X-rays by this much in total,

